// Recall-mode questions — Behavioral Science (part 1)
// Format: {s: tag, q: stem, choices: [4], ans: 0-3 index into choices, exp: explanation}
const QB_BEHAVIORAL_1 = [
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are the basic working units of the nervous system: specialized cells that transmit information to other nerve cells, muscle cells, or gland cells',choices:['Glial cells','Neurons','Synapses','Myelin sheaths'],ans:1,exp:'**Neurons** are excitable cells that conduct action potentials and release neurotransmitters onto their targets. Glial cells (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells, microglia) support, insulate and protect neurons but do not carry the signal themselves.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are circuits in which spinal cord interneurons relay sensory input directly to motor neurons, producing a response at the site of the stimulus while the signal is also routed to the brain',choices:['Motor units','Ascending tracts','Reflex arcs','Neuromuscular junctions'],ans:2,exp:'In a **reflex arc**, the response is organized in the spinal cord, so the limb moves before the brain consciously registers the stimulus (e.g., pulling a hand off a hot stove). The patellar (knee-jerk) reflex is monosynaptic; withdrawal reflexes add interneurons.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The central nervous system is made up of the _____ and _____',choices:['brain / cranial nerves','spinal cord / spinal nerves','brain / spinal cord','brain / autonomic ganglia'],ans:2,exp:'The **CNS** is the brain and spinal cord. Cranial nerves, spinal nerves and all ganglia lie outside it and belong to the peripheral nervous system, a common trap.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ nervous system consists of all nervous tissue and fibers outside the brain and spinal cord',choices:['central','peripheral','sympathetic','parasympathetic'],ans:1,exp:'The **peripheral nervous system** includes cranial and spinal nerves and ganglia, and divides into somatic (voluntary) and autonomic (involuntary) branches. The sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are only subdivisions of the autonomic branch, not all tissue outside the CNS.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The part of the nervous system responsible for the fight-or-flight response is the _____',choices:['parasympathetic nervous system','somatic nervous system','sympathetic nervous system','enteric nervous system'],ans:2,exp:'The **sympathetic** division raises heart rate, dilates pupils and bronchioles, releases glucose and diverts blood to skeletal muscle. Mnemonic: sympathetic = "stress"; the parasympathetic division does the opposite.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The part of the nervous system responsible for the rest-and-digest response is the _____',choices:['parasympathetic nervous system','sympathetic nervous system','somatic nervous system','central nervous system'],ans:0,exp:'The **parasympathetic** division (largely via the vagus nerve, using acetylcholine) slows the heart, constricts pupils and stimulates digestion. The sympathetic division drives the opposite fight-or-flight response.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are chemical messengers released from a neuron\'s axon terminal into the synaptic cleft to signal the next cell',choices:['Hormones','Cytokines','Neurotransmitters','Second messengers'],ans:2,exp:'**Neurotransmitters** cross the synaptic cleft and bind receptors on the postsynaptic cell, acting fast and locally. Hormones, by contrast, travel through the bloodstream to distant targets.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter used at the neuromuscular junction to _____',choices:['trigger skeletal muscle contraction','relax skeletal muscle','block pain signals','inhibit motor neurons'],ans:0,exp:'**Acetylcholine** released by motor neurons binds nicotinic receptors on skeletal muscle and triggers contraction. It is also the transmitter of the parasympathetic system and is depleted in Alzheimer\'s disease.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is a neurotransmitter involved in focus, motivation and reward, as well as in maintaining smooth movements and steady posture',choices:['Serotonin','Dopamine','GABA','Glycine'],ans:1,exp:'**Dopamine** from the substantia nigra supports smooth movement (its loss causes Parkinson\'s disease), and mesolimbic dopamine drives reward and motivation. Excess dopamine activity is linked to the positive symptoms of schizophrenia.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Endorphins and enkephalins are neuropeptides that act as the body\'s _____',choices:['primary excitatory transmitters','sleep-inducing hormones','natural painkillers','muscle-activating transmitters'],ans:2,exp:'**Endorphins** and **enkephalins** are endogenous opioids that bind the same receptors as morphine, reducing pain perception and producing euphoria (e.g., "runner\'s high").'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Epinephrine and norepinephrine maintain _____ and mediate the _____',choices:['sleep / rest-and-digest response','wakefulness / rest-and-digest response','wakefulness / fight-or-flight response','sleep / fight-or-flight response'],ans:2,exp:'The catecholamines **epinephrine** and **norepinephrine** promote arousal and alertness and drive the sympathetic **fight-or-flight** response. Rest-and-digest is the parasympathetic, acetylcholine-mediated state.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'γ-Aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the main _____ neurotransmitter of the brain',choices:['excitatory','inhibitory','cholinergic','monoaminergic'],ans:1,exp:'**GABA** opens Cl⁻ channels (GABA-A) and hyperpolarizes neurons, making them less likely to fire, so it is inhibitory. Benzodiazepines, barbiturates and alcohol enhance GABA-A signaling; glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Glutamate is the main _____ neurotransmitter of the brain',choices:['inhibitory','cholinergic','monoaminergic','excitatory'],ans:3,exp:'**Glutamate** opens cation channels (AMPA, NMDA) that depolarize neurons toward threshold, so it is excitatory. NMDA-receptor activity underlies long-term potentiation; GABA is the main inhibitory transmitter.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ modulates mood, sleep, eating, and dreaming',choices:['Dopamine','Glutamate','Serotonin','Epinephrine'],ans:2,exp:'**Serotonin** (from the raphe nuclei) regulates mood, appetite and sleep. Low serotonin is linked to depression, which is why SSRIs are first-line antidepressants.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is the glucocorticoid stress hormone released by the adrenal cortex',choices:['Epinephrine','Aldosterone','Cortisol','Melatonin'],ans:2,exp:'**Cortisol** is released through the HPA axis (CRH → ACTH → cortisol) and raises blood glucose while suppressing immune function. Epinephrine comes from the adrenal medulla, and aldosterone is a cortical mineralocorticoid that regulates Na⁺, not a stress glucocorticoid.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Testosterone and estrogen are sex hormones that mediate _____, among other functions',choices:['libido','circadian rhythm','blood glucose','calcium balance'],ans:0,exp:'The sex steroids **testosterone** and **estrogen** drive sexual desire (**libido**) and secondary sex characteristics. Circadian rhythm is set by melatonin, glucose by insulin and glucagon, and calcium by PTH and calcitonin.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ includes the cerebellum, the pons, and the medulla oblongata',choices:['forebrain','midbrain','hindbrain','diencephalon'],ans:2,exp:'The **hindbrain** (rhombencephalon) controls balance and coordination (cerebellum) and vital functions such as breathing and heart rate (medulla). Together with the midbrain, the pons and medulla make up the brainstem.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ includes the superior and inferior colliculi, the tegmentum, and the cerebral peduncles',choices:['hindbrain','forebrain','cerebellum','midbrain'],ans:3,exp:'The **midbrain** (mesencephalon) relays sensory and motor signals; the superior colliculi handle visual reflexes and the inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes. Mnemonic: superior = sight, inferior = ear.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ includes the thalamus, the hypothalamus, the basal ganglia, the limbic system, and the cerebral cortex',choices:['midbrain','hindbrain','forebrain','brainstem'],ans:2,exp:'The **forebrain** (prosencephalon) handles complex perception, cognition, emotion and voluntary movement. The brainstem is made of the midbrain, pons and medulla, not these structures.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is the relay station for sensory information',choices:['amygdala','hippocampus','hypothalamus','thalamus'],ans:3,exp:'The **thalamus** sorts incoming sensory signals and relays them to the correct cortical area. Smell is the one exception; olfactory input bypasses the thalamus.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ regulates homeostasis and is in charge of the 4 F\'s',choices:['thalamus','amygdala','hippocampus','hypothalamus'],ans:3,exp:'The **hypothalamus** controls feeding, fighting, fleeing and mating (the 4 F\'s), plus body temperature, thirst and the endocrine system through the pituitary. The thalamus only relays sensory information.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ smooths movements and helps maintain postural stability',choices:['basal ganglia','amygdala','hippocampus','thalamus'],ans:0,exp:'The **basal ganglia** coordinate and smooth voluntary movement and posture. Their dysfunction causes movement disorders: Parkinson\'s disease (loss of dopamine input) and Huntington\'s disease.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6C',q:'The _____ deals with emotions, memories, and arousal (or stimulation)',choices:['prefrontal cortex','brainstem','limbic system','cerebellum'],ans:2,exp:'The **limbic system** includes the amygdala (emotion, fear), hippocampus (memory), septal nuclei (pleasure) and parts of the hypothalamus. The prefrontal cortex regulates emotion but is not itself limbic.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6C',q:'The _____ is in charge of fear and aggression',choices:['amygdala','prefrontal cortex','hippocampus','hypothalamus'],ans:0,exp:'The **amygdala** attaches emotional (especially fear) significance to stimuli and drives defensive and aggressive responses. Damage reduces fear, as seen in Klüver-Bucy syndrome.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is responsible for consolidating short-term memories into long-term memories',choices:['hippocampus','hypothalamus','thalamus','amygdala'],ans:0,exp:'The **hippocampus** (medial temporal lobe) consolidates new explicit memories into long-term storage; bilateral damage causes anterograde amnesia, as in patient H.M. The amygdala handles fear, not memory consolidation.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is the outer layer of neural tissue of the cerebrum',choices:['corpus callosum','basal ganglia','white matter','cerebral cortex'],ans:3,exp:'The **cerebral cortex** is the folded gray matter (cell bodies) covering the cerebrum and handles higher functions. Beneath it lies white matter (myelinated axons), including the corpus callosum that connects the two hemispheres.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is associated with touch, pressure, temperature, pain, and spatial processing',choices:['frontal lobe','temporal lobe','parietal lobe','occipital lobe'],ans:2,exp:'The **parietal lobe** contains the primary somatosensory cortex on the postcentral gyrus and handles spatial orientation. The primary motor cortex is just in front of it, on the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is associated with executive function, impulse control, speech production, and motor control',choices:['temporal lobe','parietal lobe','occipital lobe','frontal lobe'],ans:3,exp:'The **frontal lobe** contains the prefrontal cortex (planning, impulse control), the primary motor cortex and Broca\'s area (speech production). Phineas Gage\'s personality change followed frontal damage.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is associated with vision',choices:['parietal lobe','temporal lobe','occipital lobe','frontal lobe'],ans:2,exp:'The **occipital lobe** at the back of the brain contains the primary visual cortex (striate cortex). Visual signals reach it from the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is associated with sound, speech perception, memory, and emotion',choices:['parietal lobe','occipital lobe','temporal lobe','frontal lobe'],ans:2,exp:'The **temporal lobe** contains the auditory cortex, Wernicke\'s area (language comprehension) and the hippocampus and amygdala. Speech production (Broca\'s area) is in the frontal lobe instead.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Language, logic, analytic thinking, and math are associated with the cerebral hemisphere that is _____',choices:['left, dominant in most people','right, dominant in most people','left, nondominant in most people','right, nondominant in most people'],ans:0,exp:'In most people (including most left-handers) the **left** hemisphere is **dominant** for language and analytic processing. The right, nondominant hemisphere is associated with intuition, creativity and spatial processing; this is a simplification, since the hemispheres work together.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Intuition, creativity, and spatial processing are associated with the cerebral hemisphere that is _____',choices:['left, dominant in most people','right, dominant in most people','left, nondominant in most people','right, nondominant in most people'],ans:3,exp:'The **right** hemisphere is **nondominant** in most people and is linked to spatial, holistic and emotional processing, including recognizing faces and emotional tone. The left, dominant hemisphere handles language and logic.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The rooting, Moro, Babinski, and grasping reflexes are examples of _____',choices:['conditioned responses','stretch reflexes','voluntary motor milestones','primitive reflexes'],ans:3,exp:'**Primitive reflexes** are present at birth and disappear as the cortex matures and inhibits them. Their persistence or reappearance in an adult (e.g., a positive Babinski sign) indicates neurological damage.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is when an infant turns its head toward a touch on the cheek or mouth',choices:['Moro reflex','Babinski reflex','grasping reflex','rooting reflex'],ans:3,exp:'The **rooting reflex** helps the newborn find the nipple to feed. Don\'t confuse it with the Moro reflex, which is a startle response to a falling sensation.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is when an infant extends its arms outward in response to a sudden falling sensation',choices:['rooting reflex','Babinski reflex','Moro reflex','grasping reflex'],ans:2,exp:'The **Moro reflex** is a startle response: the arms extend and spread, then pull back in. It normally disappears by about 4–6 months of age.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is when an infant\'s big toe extends and the other toes fan out when the sole of the foot is stroked',choices:['Babinski reflex','Moro reflex','rooting reflex','grasping reflex'],ans:0,exp:'The **Babinski reflex** is normal in infants because the corticospinal tracts are not yet fully myelinated. In adults, a positive Babinski sign suggests upper motor neuron damage.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ is when an infant grabs anything placed in its hands',choices:['grasping reflex','rooting reflex','Moro reflex','Babinski reflex'],ans:0,exp:'In the **grasping (palmar) reflex**, the fingers close around any object placed in the palm. Like the other primitive reflexes, it fades as the cortex matures.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ are neurons or specialized cells that respond to a specific type of stimulus and convert it into electrical signals',choices:['Sensory ganglia','Projection areas','Sensory receptors','Interneurons'],ans:2,exp:'**Sensory receptors** (photoreceptors, mechanoreceptors, chemoreceptors and so on) carry out **transduction**, converting physical stimuli into neural signals. Sensory ganglia hold the cell bodies of sensory neurons; they do not detect stimuli.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ are collections of sensory neuron cell bodies outside the CNS that appear as enlargements along peripheral nerves',choices:['Sensory ganglia','Sensory receptors','Projection areas','Nuclei'],ans:0,exp:'**Sensory ganglia** (e.g., dorsal root ganglia) contain the cell bodies of sensory neurons that carry signals into the CNS. A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS is called a nucleus, not a ganglion.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Projection areas are regions of the cerebral cortex, such as the primary visual and somatosensory cortices, where _____ is further analyzed',choices:['motor output','hormonal signaling','sensory input','autonomic feedback'],ans:2,exp:'Sensory signals pass from receptors to ganglia to **projection areas** in the cortex, where **sensory input** is analyzed further. Examples are the occipital visual cortex and the parietal somatosensory cortex.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the minimum stimulus energy that will activate a sensory system',choices:['absolute threshold','threshold of conscious perception','difference threshold','just-noticeable difference'],ans:0,exp:'The **absolute threshold** is the weakest stimulus that activates the sensory system, even if the person is not consciously aware of it. A stimulus below the threshold of conscious perception is called subliminal.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the minimum stimulus energy that will create a signal large enough in size and long enough in duration to be brought into awareness',choices:['absolute threshold','difference threshold','just-noticeable difference','threshold of conscious perception'],ans:3,exp:'The **threshold of conscious perception** is the level at which a stimulus is actually noticed. A stimulus can exceed the absolute threshold (activating receptors) yet stay below this threshold, making it subliminal.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the minimum difference in magnitude between two stimuli before one can perceive the difference',choices:['sensory adaptation','difference threshold','threshold of conscious perception','absolute threshold'],ans:1,exp:'The **difference threshold** is also called the just-noticeable difference (JND). The absolute threshold is about detecting a single stimulus at all, not telling two stimuli apart.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Weber\'s law states that the just-noticeable difference for a stimulus is proportional to the _____ of the stimulus',choices:['duration','magnitude','absolute threshold','rate of adaptation'],ans:1,exp:'Under **Weber\'s law**, ΔI/I = k, so the JND is a constant fraction of the original stimulus **magnitude**. If you can just tell 100 g from 102 g, you need 204 g to notice a change from 200 g.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ refers to the effects of nonsensory factors, such as experiences, motives, and expectations, on perception of stimuli',choices:['Weber\'s law','Sensory adaptation','Bottom-up processing','Signal detection theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Signal detection theory** explains why the same faint stimulus is sometimes reported and sometimes not, depending on expectations and motivation. Its outcomes are hits, misses, false alarms and correct negatives.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ is a change in receptor sensitivity with continued exposure that can increase or decrease our sensitivity to a stimulus',choices:['Transduction','Signal detection','Sensory adaptation','Parallel processing'],ans:2,exp:'In **sensory adaptation**, receptors become less responsive to a constant stimulus (no longer noticing a smell) or more sensitive under changed conditions (the pupils dilate in the dark). Transduction is the conversion of a stimulus into a neural signal, not a change in sensitivity.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ gathers and filters incoming light',choices:['iris','cornea','lens','retina'],ans:1,exp:'The **cornea** is the transparent front surface of the eye; it gathers and filters light and provides most of the eye\'s refractive power. The lens adds adjustable fine focusing.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ controls the size of the pupil',choices:['iris','cornea','ciliary body','lens'],ans:0,exp:'The **iris** has a sympathetic dilator and a parasympathetic constrictor muscle that set pupil size. The ciliary body changes the shape of the lens instead.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ changes shape through contraction of the ciliary muscle to fine-tune the focus of light on the retina',choices:['cornea','lens','iris','pupil'],ans:1,exp:'The **lens** adjusts its curvature (**accommodation**) to focus near or far objects on the retina. The cornea refracts more light overall, but its shape is fixed.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ is the clear, watery fluid that fills the space between the cornea and lens of the eye and helps maintain intraocular pressure',choices:['Vitreous humor','Aqueous humor','Tear film','Synovial fluid'],ans:1,exp:'**Aqueous humor** is made by the ciliary body and drains through the canal of Schlemm; blocked drainage raises pressure and causes glaucoma. Vitreous humor is the gel behind the lens.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the sensory membrane that lines the back of the eye',choices:['choroid','sclera','retina','cornea'],ans:2,exp:'The **retina** contains the photoreceptors (rods and cones) plus bipolar and ganglion cells. The choroid is the vascular layer behind it, and the sclera is the tough white outer coat.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Rods are photoreceptors in the retina that detect _____',choices:['dim light','color','fine detail','depth'],ans:0,exp:'**Rods** contain rhodopsin and are highly sensitive to **dim light** (night vision) but give only black-and-white, low-detail images. They are concentrated in the peripheral retina.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Cones are photoreceptors in the retina that detect _____',choices:['dim light','color','depth','peripheral motion'],ans:1,exp:'**Cones** come in three types (short, medium and long wavelength) that detect **color** and fine detail in bright light. They are concentrated in the fovea; rods handle dim light and peripheral vision.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ provides a binocular cue that facilitates depth perception',choices:['Relative size','Interposition','Retinal disparity','Motion parallax'],ans:2,exp:'**Retinal disparity** is the slight difference between the images on the two retinas, which the brain uses to judge depth; convergence is the other binocular cue. Relative size, interposition and motion parallax are all monocular cues.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Horizontal and amacrine cells integrate signals from multiple photoreceptors and bipolar cells before they reach the ganglion cells, performing _____',choices:['phototransduction','edge-sharpening','dark adaptation','pupillary constriction'],ans:1,exp:'**Horizontal and amacrine cells** use lateral inhibition across neighboring cells to sharpen contrast at edges before signals leave the retina via ganglion cells. Phototransduction happens earlier, in the rods and cones.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ cells process motion',choices:['Parvocellular','Magnocellular','Bipolar','Horizontal'],ans:1,exp:'**Magnocellular** cells have high temporal resolution and detect motion, but they have low spatial resolution. Mnemonic: magno = motion; parvocellular cells handle shape, color and fine detail.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Parvocellular cells process _____',choices:['motion','shape and color','dim light','sound localization'],ans:1,exp:'**Parvocellular** cells have high spatial resolution and color sensitivity but low temporal resolution, so they handle **shape** and color. Motion is processed by magnocellular cells.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ is the idea that the visual system processes color, form, and motion simultaneously',choices:['Feature detection','Serial processing','Parallel processing','Bottom-up processing'],ans:2,exp:'**Parallel processing** means several aspects of a stimulus are analyzed at the same time by separate pathways. Feature detection is a related idea: specialized cells each detect one feature (e.g., magnocellular cells for motion), but it does not mean simultaneous processing.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The item missing from the visual pathway: eye → optic nerves → optic chiasm → optic tracts → _____ → visual radiations → visual cortex',choices:['medial geniculate nucleus (MGN)','lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN)','superior colliculus','pulvinar nucleus'],ans:1,exp:'The **lateral geniculate nucleus** of the thalamus relays visual signals to the occipital cortex. Mnemonic: lateral = light, medial = music; the MGN relays auditory signals.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The three main divisions of the ear are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['outer / inner / central','pinna / ossicles / cochlea','external / cochlear / vestibular','outer / middle / inner'],ans:3,exp:'The ear has **outer**, **middle** and **inner** divisions. Sound passes from the outer ear (pinna, canal, tympanic membrane) to the middle ear (ossicles) to the inner ear (cochlea, vestibular system); the pinna, ossicles and cochlea are structures within those divisions.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The outer ear consists of the _____',choices:['pinna, external auditory canal, and tympanic membrane','malleus, incus, and stapes','cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals','pinna, ossicles, and oval window'],ans:0,exp:'The **outer ear** collects sound and channels it to the **tympanic membrane** (eardrum), which marks the boundary with the middle ear. The ossicles belong to the middle ear.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The middle ear contains the _____',choices:['pinna, external auditory canal, and tympanic membrane','malleus, incus, and stapes','cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals','utricle, saccule, and cochlea'],ans:1,exp:'The **ossicles** (malleus, incus, stapes) of the middle ear amplify vibrations from the tympanic membrane and pass them to the oval window. The eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the throat to equalize pressure.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The malleus is one of three ossicles in the middle ear and is the _____',choices:['anvil','stirrup','hammer','drum'],ans:2,exp:'The **malleus (hammer)** attaches to the tympanic membrane and passes vibrations on to the incus. The order is hammer → anvil → stirrup (malleus → incus → stapes).'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The incus is one of three ossicles in the middle ear and is the _____',choices:['hammer','stirrup','drum','anvil'],ans:3,exp:'The **incus (anvil)** is the middle ossicle, between the malleus and the stapes. Order: hammer → anvil → stirrup.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The stapes is one of three ossicles in the middle ear and is the _____',choices:['stirrup','hammer','anvil','drum'],ans:0,exp:'The **stapes (stirrup)** is the smallest bone in the body; its footplate pushes on the oval window of the cochlea. Order: hammer → anvil → stirrup.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The inner ear contains the _____',choices:['malleus, incus, and stapes','cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals','pinna, external auditory canal, and tympanic membrane','ossicles, oval window, and eustachian tube'],ans:1,exp:'The **inner ear** is a fluid-filled bony labyrinth: the **cochlea** handles hearing, while the vestibule (utricle and saccule, linear acceleration) and the semicircular canals (rotation) handle balance.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the cartilaginous outer flap of the ear that funnels sound into the external auditory canal',choices:['tympanic membrane','oval window','eustachian tube','pinna (auricle)'],ans:3,exp:'The **pinna (auricle)** collects sound waves and helps with sound localization by filtering them. The tympanic membrane, at the end of the canal, vibrates in response.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ carries sound from the pinna to the eardrum',choices:['eustachian tube','external auditory canal','oval window','round window'],ans:1,exp:'The **external auditory canal** is the passage of the outer ear that leads to the tympanic membrane. The eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the throat to equalize pressure; it does not carry sound.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is a thin membrane stretched tight like a drum that vibrates when sound hits it',choices:['tympanic membrane','oval window','basilar membrane','tectorial membrane'],ans:0,exp:'The **tympanic membrane** (eardrum) separates the outer ear from the middle ear and passes vibrations to the ossicles. The basilar membrane in the cochlea also vibrates, but it lies in the inner ear and carries the hair cells.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The cochlea transduces sound vibrations into _____ to be sent to the brain',choices:['light signals','hormonal signals','nerve impulses','pressure waves'],ans:2,exp:'In the cochlea, vibration of the basilar membrane bends hair cells and converts mechanical energy into **nerve impulses** carried by the auditory (vestibulocochlear) nerve. High frequencies are detected at the base and low frequencies at the apex (place theory).'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is a sensory system that gives the brain information about motion, head position, and spatial orientation, and is involved in the motor functions that keep us balanced',choices:['proprioceptive system','vestibular system','somatosensory system','auditory system'],ans:1,exp:'The **vestibular system** of the inner ear (semicircular canals plus utricle and saccule) detects head rotation and linear acceleration. Proprioception is different: it reports limb and body position from receptors in muscles and joints.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The semicircular canals are responsible for _____',choices:['sensing rotational acceleration for balance','detecting sound frequency','amplifying sound vibrations','equalizing middle ear pressure'],ans:0,exp:'The three fluid-filled **semicircular canals** are set at right angles; endolymph moving within them signals **rotational** acceleration of the head. The utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration, and the cochlea detects sound frequency.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The superior olive is located in the _____ and its function is to _____',choices:['brainstem / localize sound','midbrain / detect visual motion','thalamus / relay auditory information','brainstem / amplify sound vibrations'],ans:0,exp:'The **superior olive** in the brainstem compares the timing and loudness of sound at the two ears to **localize sound**. The thalamic relay for hearing is the medial geniculate nucleus, and the ossicles amplify vibrations.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is a midbrain auditory relay that is also involved in the startle reflex',choices:['superior colliculus','inferior colliculus','superior olive','medial geniculate nucleus'],ans:1,exp:'The **inferior colliculus** in the midbrain processes auditory information and mediates the startle reflex to sudden sounds. Mnemonic: superior colliculus = sight (visual reflexes), inferior colliculus = ear.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ keeps the eyes fixed on a single point as the head rotates',choices:['pupillary light reflex','accommodation reflex','vestibulo-ocular reflex','optokinetic nystagmus'],ans:2,exp:'The **vestibulo-ocular reflex** uses input from the semicircular canals to move the eyes opposite to head rotation, keeping the image stable on the retina. The pupillary light reflex changes pupil size, not eye position.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Smell is the detection of volatile or aerosolized chemicals by the _____ chemoreceptors',choices:['visual','auditory','gustatory','olfactory'],ans:3,exp:'**Olfactory** receptors in the nasal epithelium send signals to the olfactory bulb, which projects directly to limbic areas without passing through the thalamus. Gustatory receptors detect dissolved chemicals (taste), not airborne ones.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ are chemicals released by animals that affect the social, foraging, and sexual behavior of others of the same species',choices:['Hormones','Neurotransmitters','Pheromones','Odorants'],ans:2,exp:'**Pheromones** are detected mainly by the vomeronasal organ in many animals and carry signals between individuals. Hormones act within the body that makes them, not between members of a species.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The five basic taste categories are _____, _____, _____, _____, and _____',choices:['sweet / sour / salty / bitter / spicy','sweet / sour / salty / bitter / umami','sweet / sour / salty / bitter / astringent','sweet / sour / hot / bitter / umami'],ans:1,exp:'The five tastes are **sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami** (savory, from glutamate). Spiciness and heat come from pain and temperature receptors (capsaicin acting on TRPV1), not from taste receptors.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ refers to the four touch modalities: pressure, vibration, pain, and temperature',choices:['Proprioception','Kinesthesia','Somatosensation','Nociception'],ans:2,exp:'**Somatosensation** covers the skin senses of pressure, vibration, pain and temperature. Nociception is pain alone, and proprioception and kinesthesia refer to body position and movement.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ is the minimum distance needed between two points of stimulation on the skin for them to be felt as two distinct stimuli',choices:['difference threshold','absolute threshold','two-point threshold','threshold of conscious perception'],ans:2,exp:'The **two-point threshold** depends on receptor density; it is smallest on the fingertips and lips and largest on the back. The difference threshold is about the magnitude of a stimulus, not the spacing between two points.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ is the temperature that the skin perceives as neither warm nor cold',choices:['Absolute threshold','Physiological zero','Basal temperature','Sensory adaptation level'],ans:1,exp:'**Physiological zero** is the neutral skin temperature (roughly 30–33 °C, varying with body site) against which warmth and cold are judged. It can shift with adaptation, which is why lukewarm water feels hot after your hand has been in cold water.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Nociceptors sense _____',choices:['pressure','temperature','pain','body position'],ans:2,exp:'**Nociceptors** are free nerve endings that respond to tissue-damaging stimuli and signal pain. Thermoreceptors sense temperature, and proprioceptors sense body position.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ of pain asserts that non-painful input closes the nerve gates to painful input, which prevents pain sensation from traveling to the central nervous system',choices:['opponent process theory','signal detection theory','gate control theory','Weber\'s law'],ans:2,exp:'In **gate control theory**, input from large touch fibers activates spinal interneurons that block pain transmission, which is why rubbing a bumped elbow helps. Opponent process theory explains color vision and emotional after-reactions, not pain.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ is the sense that tells you about your body\'s position in space',choices:['Nociception','Proprioception','Thermoception','Interoception'],ans:1,exp:'**Proprioception** relies on muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs and joint receptors to track limb position without looking. Interoception senses internal body states such as hunger or heartbeat.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Top-down processing is the recognition of an object based on _____',choices:['individual sensory features','memories and expectations','raw sensory input alone','reflexive responses'],ans:1,exp:'**Top-down processing** starts from prior knowledge and **expectations**, then interprets incoming sensations (e.g., reading a word with a missing letter). Building a percept from individual features is bottom-up processing.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Using bottom-up processing, one will process the _____ first, then the _____',choices:['whole / details','details / whole','expectations / details','whole / expectations'],ans:1,exp:'**Bottom-up processing** starts with the **details** (features detected by specialized neurons) and assembles them into a **whole** object. Starting from the whole or from expectations is top-down processing.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'_____ are principles that describe how the mind groups perceptual elements into whole objects',choices:['Gestalt principles','Feature detectors','Heuristics','Schemas'],ans:0,exp:'**Gestalt principles** (proximity, similarity, continuity, closure, Prägnanz) describe how we organize parts into wholes, so the whole is perceived as more than the sum of its parts. Heuristics are shortcuts for judgment, not for perceptual grouping.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'According to the gestalt principle of _____, objects that are placed close together are perceived as being more related than those spaced farther apart',choices:['similarity','closure','continuity','proximity'],ans:3,exp:'**Proximity** is about spatial nearness: dots grouped closely look like a set. Similarity groups items by shared appearance (color, shape) instead.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'According to the gestalt principle of _____, we tend to perceive things that physically resemble one another as part of the same object',choices:['similarity','closure','continuity','proximity'],ans:0,exp:'**Similarity** groups elements with shared color, shape or size, such as seeing rows of same-colored dots. Proximity groups by distance rather than appearance.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'According to the gestalt principle of _____, we perceive lines and curves as following the smoothest continuous path rather than as abrupt changes in direction',choices:['proximity','closure','similarity','continuity'],ans:3,exp:'**Continuity** (good continuation) is why two crossing lines look like an X rather than two Vs touching at their points. Closure is a different principle: filling gaps to complete a shape.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'According to the gestalt principle of _____, we tend to see complete figures even when part of the information is missing',choices:['continuity','similarity','closure','proximity'],ans:2,exp:'**Closure** makes us see a full circle in a ring with gaps, because the mind fills in the missing parts. Continuity is about following smooth lines, not completing shapes.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'The _____ states that people will perceive and interpret ambiguous or complex images as the simplest form(s) possible',choices:['law of proximity','law of Prägnanz','law of closure','law of similarity'],ans:1,exp:'The **law of Prägnanz** ("good figure") is the overarching gestalt principle: we perceive the simplest, most stable interpretation, such as seeing the Olympic logo as five overlapping circles rather than many curved fragments.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ is when one becomes used to a repeated stimulus and responds to it less over time',choices:['Sensitization','Dishabituation','Habituation','Extinction'],ans:2,exp:'**Habituation** is a simple non-associative form of learning (e.g., no longer noticing traffic noise). Sensitization is the opposite: an increased response to a repeated (often noxious) stimulus.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ may occur after habituation, when a second stimulus intervenes and causes a renewed response to the original stimulus',choices:['Sensitization','Dishabituation','Habituation','Spontaneous recovery'],ans:1,exp:'**Dishabituation** restores the response to a habituated stimulus after a new stimulus intervenes; it shows that habituation is not sensory fatigue. Spontaneous recovery is a different thing: an extinguished conditioned response reappearing after a delay.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'The _____ theory states that ideas reinforce each other and can be linked to one another',choices:['social learning','associative learning','drive reduction','information processing'],ans:1,exp:'**Associative learning** pairs stimuli with each other (classical conditioning) or behaviors with consequences (operant conditioning). Social learning theory emphasizes learning by observing others instead.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ is a method of learning that occurs through rewards and punishments for behavior',choices:['Classical conditioning','Operant conditioning','Observational learning','Latent learning'],ans:1,exp:'**Operant conditioning** (B.F. Skinner) links voluntary behavior to its consequences. Classical conditioning (Pavlov) instead pairs two stimuli to produce a reflexive response.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ increases the likelihood of a behavior',choices:['Punishment','Extinction','Habituation','Reinforcement'],ans:3,exp:'**Reinforcement**, positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something aversive), always increases behavior. Negative reinforcement is often confused with punishment, but punishment decreases behavior.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ decreases the likelihood of a behavior',choices:['Negative reinforcement','Shaping','Positive reinforcement','Punishment'],ans:3,exp:'**Punishment** reduces a behavior, either by adding something aversive (positive punishment) or by removing something pleasant (negative punishment). Negative reinforcement removes something aversive, so it increases behavior.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'Schedules of reinforcement can be based on _____ or _____ (each of which can be fixed or variable)',choices:['rewards / punishments','primary reinforcers / secondary reinforcers','ratios / intervals','acquisition / extinction'],ans:2,exp:'**Ratio** schedules reinforce after a number of responses and **interval** schedules after an amount of time, giving FR, VR, FI and VI. Variable-ratio schedules produce the highest response rates and the most resistance to extinction (e.g., slot machines).'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'In operant conditioning, "positive" (as in positive reinforcement or positive punishment) means to _____ a stimulus',choices:['remove','add','strengthen','weaken'],ans:1,exp:'**Positive** means a stimulus is **added**, and negative means one is removed. Whether behavior increases or decreases is what separates reinforcement from punishment; "positive" does not mean "good".'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'In operant conditioning, "negative" (as in negative reinforcement or negative punishment) means to _____ a stimulus',choices:['remove','add','strengthen','weaken'],ans:0,exp:'**Negative** means a stimulus is **removed**. For example, taking an aspirin to remove a headache is negative reinforcement, and losing phone privileges is negative punishment.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'When a previously reinforced behavior is no longer reinforced and gradually stops occurring, it has undergone _____',choices:['punishment','habituation','extinction','spontaneous recovery'],ans:2,exp:'In operant conditioning, **extinction** occurs when reinforcement stops (in classical conditioning, when the CS is no longer paired with the US). The behavior may briefly return later, which is spontaneous recovery.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ is reinforcing behaviors that come closer and closer to the target behavior',choices:['Chaining','Shaping','Generalization','Discrimination'],ans:1,exp:'**Shaping** reinforces **successive approximations** of a target behavior, such as training an animal step by step. Generalization is responding to stimuli similar to the original one.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'In classical conditioning, a _____ becomes a _____ that produces a conditioned response',choices:['neutral stimulus / conditioned stimulus','unconditioned stimulus / conditioned stimulus','conditioned stimulus / unconditioned stimulus','neutral stimulus / unconditioned stimulus'],ans:0,exp:'Repeated pairing with an unconditioned stimulus turns a **neutral stimulus** (Pavlov\'s bell) into a **conditioned stimulus** that alone triggers salivation. The unconditioned stimulus (food) causes its response innately and never becomes conditioned.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'7C',q:'_____ is the acquisition of behavior by watching others',choices:['Operant conditioning','Observational learning (modeling)','Classical conditioning','Latent learning'],ans:1,exp:'**Observational learning (modeling)** was shown in Bandura\'s Bobo doll experiment, where children copied adults\' aggression. Latent learning is learning that happens without reinforcement and only shows when an incentive appears.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the process of putting new information into memory',choices:['Storage','Encoding','Retrieval','Consolidation'],ans:1,exp:'**Encoding** is the first stage of memory: converting experience into a form that can be stored. Storage keeps the information, and retrieval brings it back out.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is a type of encoding in which the meaning of something is encoded, as opposed to its sound or appearance',choices:['Acoustic encoding','Visual encoding','Semantic encoding','Maintenance rehearsal'],ans:2,exp:'**Semantic encoding** is the deepest level of processing and gives the best recall, especially when the information is related to oneself (self-reference effect). Visual encoding is the weakest, and acoustic encoding is intermediate.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ and _____ are transient and based on neurotransmitter activity',choices:['Long-term memory / implicit memory','Sensory memory / long-term memory','Procedural memory / semantic memory','Sensory memory / short-term memory'],ans:3,exp:'**Sensory memory** (under 1 second for iconic memory) and **short-term memory** (about 30 seconds, 7 ± 2 items) are brief neurochemical activity. Long-term memory needs structural change at synapses, such as long-term potentiation.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is responsible for temporarily holding and manipulating information that is available for processing',choices:['Sensory memory','Long-term memory','Working memory','Procedural memory'],ans:2,exp:'**Working memory** (Baddeley: phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, central executive) actively manipulates information, as in mental arithmetic. Sensory memory only stores raw input for an instant.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is responsible for the storage of information for an extended period of time',choices:['Short-term memory','Working memory','Sensory memory','Long-term memory'],ans:3,exp:'**Long-term memory** has essentially unlimited capacity and duration and is divided into explicit (declarative) and implicit (nondeclarative) memory. Information reaches it through rehearsal and elaborative encoding.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ accounts for memories that we must consciously recall with effort and focus',choices:['Implicit (nondeclarative) memory','Explicit (declarative) memory','Procedural memory','Priming'],ans:1,exp:'**Explicit (declarative) memory** includes episodic memory (personal events) and semantic memory (facts) and depends on the hippocampus. Procedural memory and priming are implicit and operate without conscious recall.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ memory is acquired and used unconsciously',choices:['Explicit','Episodic','Semantic','Implicit'],ans:3,exp:'**Implicit memory** includes procedural skills (riding a bike), priming and classically conditioned responses. Episodic and semantic memory are both explicit (declarative) forms.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'Semantic networks link concepts together based on similar _____',choices:['sound','spelling','meaning','time of learning'],ans:2,exp:'In a **semantic network**, concepts are nodes linked by **meaning**, and activating one concept primes related ones (**spreading activation**). For example, "red" primes "fire truck" and "apple".'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the process of re-accessing events or information from the past',choices:['Encoding','Storage','Consolidation','Retrieval'],ans:3,exp:'**Retrieval** brings stored memories back into awareness through recall, recognition or relearning. Recognition is easier than free recall because the cue is supplied.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Alzheimer\'s disease is a degenerative brain disorder linked to a loss of _____ in neurons that connect to the _____',choices:['dopamine / substantia nigra','acetylcholine / cerebellum','acetylcholine / hippocampus','dopamine / hippocampus'],ans:2,exp:'**Alzheimer\'s disease** involves loss of cholinergic neurons projecting to the **hippocampus** and cortex, along with β-amyloid plaques and tau neurofibrillary tangles; cholinesterase inhibitors are used to treat it. Loss of dopamine in the substantia nigra causes Parkinson\'s disease.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'The memory loss of Korsakoff syndrome is caused by a deficiency of _____',choices:['niacin (vitamin B3)','cobalamin (vitamin B12)','thiamine (vitamin B1)','folate (vitamin B9)'],ans:2,exp:'**Thiamine (vitamin B1)** deficiency, usually from chronic alcohol use, causes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, which includes **confabulation** and both retrograde and anterograde amnesia. Niacin deficiency causes pellagra instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Agnosia is the loss of the ability to recognize _____',choices:['objects, people, or sounds','words when trying to speak','how to perform learned movements','events from before an injury'],ans:0,exp:'In **agnosia**, sensation is intact but **recognition** fails (e.g., prosopagnosia, the inability to recognize faces). Trouble producing words is aphasia, trouble performing learned movements is apraxia, and losing memories from before an injury is retrograde amnesia.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is when new learning makes you forget old memories',choices:['Proactive interference','Retroactive interference','Retrograde amnesia','Decay'],ans:1,exp:'**Retroactive interference** works backward in time: new information disrupts recall of old information (e.g., a new phone number crowding out the old one). Proactive interference is the reverse.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is when old memories interfere with learning new memories',choices:['Retroactive interference','Proactive interference','Anterograde amnesia','Decay'],ans:1,exp:'**Proactive interference** works forward: old learning disrupts new learning (e.g., writing last year\'s date in January). Mnemonic: pro = forward, retro = backward.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ states that the brain encodes, stores, and retrieves information much like a computer',choices:['levels-of-processing model','information processing model','spreading activation model','dual-coding theory'],ans:1,exp:'The **information processing model** treats thinking as a series of steps (input → encoding → storage → retrieval), like a computer. The levels-of-processing model instead says deeper, more meaningful encoding produces better memory.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s sensorimotor stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['0 / 2','2 / 7','7 / 11','0 / 3'],ans:0,exp:'In the **sensorimotor stage (0–2)**, infants learn through senses and actions and develop **object permanence**; circular reactions are typical. The preoperational stage follows at 2–7.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s preoperational stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['0 / 2','2 / 7','7 / 11','2 / 5'],ans:1,exp:'The **preoperational stage (2–7)** features symbolic thinking, pretend play and **egocentrism**, but no **conservation**. Conservation develops in the concrete operational stage (7–11).'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s concrete operational stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['2 / 7','0 / 2','7 / 11','12 / 18'],ans:2,exp:'In the **concrete operational stage (7–11)**, children understand **conservation** and others\' viewpoints and can reason logically about concrete objects. Abstract reasoning waits for the formal operational stage (12+).'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s formal operational stage begins at about age _____',choices:['2','7','12','18'],ans:2,exp:'The **formal operational stage (about 12 and older)** brings **abstract** and hypothetical reasoning, such as testing hypotheses systematically. Age 7 marks the start of the concrete operational stage.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ reasoning starts with general rules, then reaches specific conclusions',choices:['Inductive','Deductive','Abductive','Analogical'],ans:1,exp:'**Deductive** (top-down) reasoning applies general premises to reach a guaranteed conclusion (e.g., all men are mortal; Socrates is a man; so Socrates is mortal). Inductive reasoning generalizes from specific evidence instead.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ reasoning starts with specific evidence, then forms general conclusions',choices:['Inductive','Deductive','Abductive','Analogical'],ans:0,exp:'**Inductive** (bottom-up) reasoning generalizes from specific examples, so its conclusions are probable rather than certain. Deductive reasoning runs the other way, from general rules to specific cases.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'A _____ is a framework for thinking about a problem, often the tendency to reuse an approach that worked before',choices:['heuristic','algorithm','mental set','insight'],ans:2,exp:'A **mental set** speeds up familiar problems but can block new solutions when the old approach no longer fits. Functional fixedness is a specific type of mental set that applies to how objects are used.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the tendency to use objects only in the way they are normally used',choices:['Mental set','Functional fixedness','Confirmation bias','Belief perseverance'],ans:1,exp:'**Functional fixedness** stops people from seeing new uses for objects, as in Duncker\'s candle problem (the tack box can serve as a shelf). Mental set is the broader tendency to repeat an old problem-solving approach.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ are mental shortcuts that allow people to solve problems and make judgments quickly and efficiently',choices:['Algorithms','Heuristics','Schemas','Insights'],ans:1,exp:'**Heuristics** are fast rules of thumb but can lead to bias; examples are the availability and representativeness heuristics. Algorithms are slow, step-by-step procedures that guarantee a solution.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is a mental shortcut that relies on the examples that come to mind most easily when evaluating a situation',choices:['representativeness heuristic','availability heuristic','anchoring bias','confirmation bias'],ans:1,exp:'The **availability heuristic** judges frequency by how easily examples come to mind, such as overestimating plane-crash risk after news coverage. The representativeness heuristic judges by similarity to a prototype instead.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is a mental shortcut that helps us make a decision by comparing information to our mental prototypes',choices:['availability heuristic','representativeness heuristic','anchoring bias','confirmation bias'],ans:1,exp:'The **representativeness heuristic** classifies things by how well they match a prototype, which can lead us to ignore **base rates** (base-rate fallacy). The availability heuristic depends on how easily examples come to mind, not on similarity.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the tendency to focus on information that fits one\'s beliefs while rejecting information that goes against those beliefs',choices:['Hindsight bias','Confirmation bias','Anchoring bias','Framing effect'],ans:1,exp:'**Confirmation bias** means seeking out and favoring evidence that supports what we already believe. Hindsight bias is a different error: believing after the fact that an outcome was predictable.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ divides human intelligence into specific modalities (e.g., linguistic, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal) rather than seeing it as one general ability',choices:['Spearman\'s general intelligence (g) theory','Gardner\'s theory of multiple intelligences','Sternberg\'s triarchic theory','Cattell\'s fluid and crystallized theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Gardner\'s theory of multiple intelligences** lists independent intelligences (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, naturalist). Spearman\'s theory is the opposite view: a single general factor (g) underlies all abilities.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ refers to the actual sound of speech',choices:['Morphology','Phonology','Semantics','Syntax'],ans:1,exp:'**Phonology** studies speech sounds (phonemes). Infants can distinguish all phonemes at first but narrow to their native language\'s phonemes within the first year.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ refers to the building blocks of words',choices:['Morphology','Phonology','Syntax','Pragmatics'],ans:0,exp:'**Morphology** covers morphemes, the smallest units of meaning (e.g., "re-" + "write" + "-ing"). Phonology covers sounds, which carry no meaning by themselves.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ refers to the meaning of words',choices:['Syntax','Semantics','Phonology','Pragmatics'],ans:1,exp:'**Semantics** is word and sentence meaning. Pragmatics is how social context changes meaning (e.g., "Can you pass the salt?" as a request).'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ refers to the rules dictating word order',choices:['Semantics','Morphology','Pragmatics','Syntax'],ans:3,exp:'**Syntax** is grammar and word order: "dog bites man" means something different from "man bites dog". Semantics deals with meaning, not word order.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ refers to the way language use changes depending on social context',choices:['Semantics','Syntax','Morphology','Pragmatics'],ans:3,exp:'**Pragmatics** covers context-dependent features such as politeness, tone, turn-taking and code-switching. Semantics is the literal meaning of words, which stays the same whatever the context.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ of language states that language acquisition is innate',choices:['behaviorist theory','social interactionist theory','nativist theory','linguistic relativity theory'],ans:2,exp:'The **nativist theory** (Chomsky) proposes an innate **language acquisition device** and a critical period for learning language. The behaviorist theory (Skinner) instead credits reinforcement.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ of language states that language acquisition is controlled by operant conditioning and reinforcement by parents and caregivers',choices:['nativist theory','behaviorist theory','social interactionist theory','linguistic relativity theory'],ans:1,exp:'The **behaviorist theory** (Skinner) says that babbling sounds which parents reinforce are kept. It struggles to explain how quickly children produce novel sentences, a point nativists stress.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ of language states that language acquisition is caused by a motivation to communicate and interact with others',choices:['nativist theory','behaviorist theory','linguistic relativity theory','social interactionist theory'],ans:3,exp:'The **social interactionist theory** (Vygotsky) combines biology and social interaction: children learn language because they want to communicate with caregivers. Nativist theory instead stresses an innate language device.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____, also known as the _____, states that the lens through which we view and interpret the world is created by language',choices:['nativist theory / language acquisition device','Whorfian hypothesis / linguistic relativity hypothesis','behaviorist theory / operant conditioning','Whorfian hypothesis / critical period hypothesis'],ans:1,exp:'The **Whorfian (Sapir-Whorf) hypothesis**, or **linguistic relativity hypothesis**, says language shapes perception; for example, languages with more color terms may change how colors are categorized. The critical period hypothesis is about the timing of language acquisition, not about language shaping thought.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the brain region responsible for speech production',choices:['The angular gyrus','Wernicke\'s area','The arcuate fasciculus','Broca\'s area'],ans:3,exp:'**Broca\'s area** (inferior frontal gyrus, usually in the left hemisphere) controls the motor planning of speech. Damage causes slow, effortful, nonfluent speech with comprehension intact.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the brain region in charge of language comprehension',choices:['Broca\'s area','The angular gyrus','Wernicke\'s area','The arcuate fasciculus'],ans:2,exp:'**Wernicke\'s area** (superior temporal gyrus) is where spoken and written language is understood. Damage causes fluent but meaningless speech ("word salad"). Mnemonic: Broca = broken speech, Wernicke = wordy nonsense.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is the white-matter tract that connects Broca\'s area and Wernicke\'s area',choices:['corpus callosum','arcuate fasciculus','internal capsule','fornix'],ans:1,exp:'The **arcuate fasciculus** links the language comprehension and production areas; damage to it causes conduction aphasia. The corpus callosum connects the two hemispheres, not these two areas.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Difficulty producing speech, with comprehension intact, is called _____',choices:['Wernicke\'s aphasia','conduction aphasia','Broca\'s aphasia','global aphasia'],ans:2,exp:'**Broca\'s (expressive) aphasia** produces halting, telegraphic speech, and patients are aware of and frustrated by their errors. In Wernicke\'s aphasia speech is fluent but comprehension is impaired.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Loss of speech comprehension, with fluent but often meaningless speech, is called _____',choices:['Broca\'s aphasia','Wernicke\'s aphasia','conduction aphasia','global aphasia'],ans:1,exp:'**Wernicke\'s (receptive) aphasia** results from damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus, and patients are often unaware of their errors. Broca\'s aphasia is the reverse: nonfluent speech with comprehension intact.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Someone with _____ can produce and understand speech but cannot repeat words',choices:['Broca\'s aphasia','Wernicke\'s aphasia','global aphasia','conduction aphasia'],ans:3,exp:'**Conduction aphasia** comes from damage to the arcuate fasciculus, which disconnects comprehension from production while sparing both. Global aphasia impairs both production and comprehension.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the state of being awake and able to think and respond',choices:['Arousal','Alertness','Daydreaming','Hypnosis'],ans:1,exp:'**Alertness** is the waking, attentive state of consciousness, maintained by the reticular formation and shown by beta and alpha waves on EEG. Daydreaming and hypnosis are altered states with reduced external focus.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'An EEG shows _____ waves when a person is alert or concentrating',choices:['alpha','beta','theta','delta'],ans:1,exp:'**Beta waves** are high-frequency, low-amplitude, desynchronized waves seen during active thinking. Alpha waves appear when awake but relaxed with the eyes closed. Mnemonic for wake to deep sleep: BATS-D (beta, alpha, theta, sleep spindles, delta).'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'An EEG shows _____ waves when a person is awake but relaxed, with the eyes closed',choices:['beta','delta','theta','alpha'],ans:3,exp:'**Alpha waves** are slower and more synchronized than beta waves and disappear when the eyes open or attention sharpens. Theta waves mark light sleep (N1).'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is a state in which individuals appear to be in normal control of their faculties but are highly suggestible',choices:['Meditation','Hypnosis','Daydreaming','REM sleep'],ans:1,exp:'**Hypnosis** produces a focused, relaxed state with **heightened suggestibility**; it is used for pain control and behavior change. Meditation also quiets the mind but does not increase suggestibility.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ is the practice of focusing attention on a single point of reference to quiet the mind',choices:['Hypnosis','Daydreaming','Meditation','Selective attention'],ans:2,exp:'**Meditation** produces EEG changes similar to light sleep (more alpha and theta waves) and lowers stress responses. Hypnosis differs because it involves heightened suggestibility.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Alcohol, barbiturates, and benzodiazepines are _____',choices:['stimulants','opioids','depressants','hallucinogens'],ans:2,exp:'These **depressants** enhance GABA-A receptor activity, causing sedation and relaxation. Combining them is dangerous because their effects on breathing add up.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Amphetamines, cocaine, and ecstasy (MDMA) are _____',choices:['depressants','opioids','hallucinogens','stimulants'],ans:3,exp:'**Stimulants** increase synaptic dopamine, norepinephrine and serotonin, either by blocking reuptake (cocaine) or by increasing release (amphetamines, MDMA). MDMA is also a hallucinogen, but it is classed with the stimulants on the MCAT.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Opiates and opioids can cause death by _____',choices:['cardiac arrhythmia','seizures','respiratory depression','hyperthermia'],ans:2,exp:'Opioids act on μ-opioid receptors in the brainstem and suppress the drive to breathe; **respiratory depression** is the main cause of overdose death. Naloxone reverses it.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'LSD, peyote, mescaline, and ketamine are considered _____',choices:['hallucinogens','stimulants','depressants','opioids'],ans:0,exp:'**Hallucinogens** distort perception and sensation; LSD and mescaline act on serotonin receptors, and ketamine blocks NMDA receptors (a dissociative). They are not mainly addictive through the reward pathway.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ pathway mediates drug addiction',choices:['nigrostriatal','mesolimbic','tuberoinfundibular','corticospinal'],ans:1,exp:'The **mesolimbic** dopamine pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and is the brain\'s reward circuit that addictive drugs hijack. The nigrostriatal pathway controls movement and degenerates in Parkinson\'s disease.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'During NREM stage 1 (N1) sleep, _____ waves appear on EEG',choices:['alpha','beta','theta','delta'],ans:2,exp:'**N1** is light sleep with **theta waves**, and hypnagogic hallucinations and hypnic jerks may occur. Delta waves are characteristic of N3 (slow-wave sleep).'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Sleep spindles and K complexes appear during sleep stage _____',choices:['N1','N2','N3','REM'],ans:1,exp:'**N2** shows theta waves interrupted by **sleep spindles** (bursts of fast activity) and **K complexes** (large single waves), which help keep a person asleep. It takes up the largest share of total sleep time.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Slow-wave sleep (SWS) corresponds to sleep stage _____',choices:['N1','N2','N3','REM'],ans:2,exp:'**N3** is slow-wave sleep, dominated by high-amplitude, low-frequency **delta waves**; current AASM staging merged the older stages 3 and 4 into N3. Sleepwalking, night terrors and growth hormone release are linked to this stage.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'During _____, the EEG looks like that of an awake person, but the person is asleep',choices:['N1 sleep','N2 sleep','N3 (slow-wave) sleep','REM sleep'],ans:3,exp:'**REM sleep** is called paradoxical sleep: the EEG shows beta-like waves, eye movements and vivid dreams, but the skeletal muscles are paralyzed (atonia). N3 is the opposite extreme, with slow delta waves.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is the natural internal process that regulates the sleep-wake cycle and repeats roughly every 24 hours',choices:['ultradian rhythm','infradian rhythm','circadian rhythm','homeostatic sleep drive'],ans:2,exp:'The **circadian rhythm** is set by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which responds to light and controls melatonin release from the pineal gland. Ultradian rhythms repeat more than once a day, such as the roughly 90-minute sleep cycle.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Dreaming occurs mostly during _____ sleep',choices:['N1','N3','N2','REM'],ans:3,exp:'Most vivid, story-like dreams occur during **REM** sleep, although some dreaming happens in NREM sleep. REM periods get longer as the night goes on.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'The activation-synthesis theory states that dreams are activated in the _____ and synthesized in the _____',choices:['cortex / brainstem','thalamus / hippocampus','brainstem / cortex','brainstem / cerebellum'],ans:2,exp:'In the **activation-synthesis theory**, random neural firing in the **brainstem** (pons) is woven into a story by the **cortex**. Reversing the order is the common error, since the cortex interprets rather than generates the signals.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Dyssomnias are sleep disorders that make it difficult to _____',choices:['stay still rather than act out dreams','fall asleep, stay asleep, or avoid sleep','recall dreams after waking','tell dreams apart from reality'],ans:1,exp:'**Dyssomnias** disturb the amount or timing of sleep; examples are insomnia, narcolepsy, sleep apnea and sleep deprivation. Abnormal behaviors during sleep (sleepwalking, night terrors) are parasomnias instead.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Parasomnias are sleep disorders that cause _____',choices:['difficulty falling asleep','abnormal movements or behaviors during sleep','excessive daytime sleepiness','sudden loss of muscle tone while awake'],ans:1,exp:'**Parasomnias** include sleepwalking (somnambulism), night terrors (both in N3) and nightmares. Insomnia, hypersomnia and the cataplexy of narcolepsy are dyssomnias.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ allows one to focus on a particular stimulus while still monitoring whether background stimuli require attention',choices:['Divided attention','Selective attention','Sustained attention','Inattentional blindness'],ans:1,exp:'**Selective attention** is shown by the **cocktail party effect**: you ignore other conversations yet still notice when someone says your name. Divided attention means actively attending to several tasks at once.'},
  {s:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ uses automatic processing to attend to multiple activities at one time',choices:['Selective attention','Sustained attention','Divided attention','Inattentional blindness'],ans:2,exp:'**Divided attention** works well only when tasks are automatic (e.g., walking while talking); new or controlled tasks compete for limited resources. Selective attention focuses on one stimulus while filtering out others.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is the purpose, or driving force, behind our actions',choices:['Emotion','Motivation','Attitude','Arousal'],ans:1,exp:'**Motivation** can be intrinsic (enjoying the task itself) or extrinsic (external rewards or punishments). Emotion is a subjective feeling state, not the purpose behind an action.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are innate, fixed patterns of behavior in response to stimuli',choices:['Drives','Instincts','Habits','Incentives'],ans:1,exp:'**Instincts** are unlearned, species-typical behaviors, such as a bird building a nest. Habits are learned, and drives are internal states of tension rather than fixed behaviors.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that people perform certain behaviors because of evolutionarily programmed instincts',choices:['Drive reduction theory','Incentive theory','Arousal theory','Instinct theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Instinct theory** (William James, William McDougall) explains behavior through inborn instincts. It fell out of favor because naming a behavior an instinct does not explain it; drive reduction theory replaced it.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is the state of being awake and reactive to stimuli',choices:['Motivation','Arousal','Drive','Emotion'],ans:1,exp:'**Arousal** is a physiological and psychological state of activation, regulated partly by the reticular formation. Arousal theory holds that we seek an optimal level of it.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that optimal performance requires an optimal level of arousal',choices:['Drive reduction theory','Optimal arousal theory','Instinct theory','Incentive theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Optimal arousal theory** says people seek a medium level of arousal; the **Yerkes-Dodson law** describes the resulting inverted-U curve. Simple tasks peak at higher arousal and complex tasks at lower arousal.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are internal states of tension that produce goal-directed behaviors',choices:['Instincts','Incentives','Drives','Emotions'],ans:2,exp:'**Drives**, such as hunger and thirst, come from unmet physiological needs and push behavior to restore homeostasis. Incentives work the other way: they pull behavior from outside.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that people are motivated to take certain actions to reduce the internal tension caused by unmet needs',choices:['Incentive theory','Drive reduction theory','Instinct theory','Expectancy-value theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Drive reduction theory** (Hull) explains homeostatic behaviors such as eating when hungry. It cannot explain behaviors that increase tension, such as thrill-seeking, which arousal theory covers.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that people have certain needs arranged in a hierarchy, with some needs being more primitive or basic than others',choices:['Self-determination theory','Drive reduction theory','Expectancy-value theory','Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs'],ans:3,exp:'In **Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs**, the levels run physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualization, and lower needs generally come first. Self-determination theory does not rank needs; it names three equal ones.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is the full realization of one\'s talents and potential',choices:['Self-efficacy','Self-esteem','Self-actualization','Self-concept'],ans:2,exp:'**Self-actualization** sits at the top of Maslow\'s hierarchy and is central to humanistic psychology (Maslow, Rogers). Self-esteem is the level just below it in Maslow\'s pyramid.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is a theory of human motivation that emphasizes 3 universal needs: autonomy, competence, and relatedness',choices:['Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs','Self-determination theory','Incentive theory','Expectancy-value theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Self-determination theory** (Deci and Ryan) holds that meeting these three needs builds intrinsic motivation, and that external rewards can undermine it (the overjustification effect).'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that behavior is motivated by a desire for reinforcement or incentives',choices:['Drive reduction theory','Instinct theory','Arousal theory','Incentive theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Incentive theory** says external rewards pull behavior (e.g., working for a bonus). Drive reduction theory says internal tension pushes it.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that the amount of motivation for a task is based on the expectation of success and the value of that success',choices:['Incentive theory','Expectancy-value theory','Self-determination theory','Drive reduction theory'],ans:1,exp:'In **expectancy-value theory**, motivation ≈ expectancy × value, so if either is zero, motivation is zero. A student who values an A but believes it is impossible will not try.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ views emotions as pairs of opposites, such as fear and relief or pleasure and pain',choices:['James-Lange theory','Cannon-Bard theory','two-factor theory','opponent-process theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Opponent-process theory** (Solomon) says an initial emotion triggers an opposing after-reaction that grows with repetition; this explains drug tolerance and withdrawal. The two-factor (Schachter-Singer) theory is about arousal plus a cognitive label.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ states that after a prior gain, people become more willing to take risks because the new money is not treated as their own',choices:['gambler\'s fallacy','sunk cost fallacy','endowment effect','house money effect'],ans:3,exp:'The **house money effect** (Thaler and Johnson) shows mental accounting: winnings are gambled more freely than earned money. The sunk cost fallacy is continuing an effort because of past losses.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6B',q:'The _____ is the belief that if something happens more often than normal, it will happen less often in the future, or vice versa',choices:['hot-hand fallacy','house money effect','sunk cost fallacy','gambler\'s fallacy'],ans:3,exp:'The **gambler\'s fallacy** wrongly treats independent events as dependent, such as expecting tails after five heads. The hot-hand fallacy is the opposite error: expecting a streak to continue.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'8C',q:'The _____ is when two people act out of self-interest, although both would have been better off had they cooperated',choices:['zero-sum game','bystander effect','social loafing','prisoner\'s dilemma'],ans:3,exp:'In the **prisoner\'s dilemma**, each player\'s rational choice to defect leads to a worse joint outcome than mutual cooperation. In a zero-sum game, one side\'s gain is exactly the other\'s loss, so there is no benefit to cooperating.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'_____ is a state of mind, or feeling, that is subjectively experienced based on circumstances, mood, and relationships',choices:['Motivation','Attitude','Emotion','Stress'],ans:2,exp:'**Emotion** is a short-lived feeling with cognitive, physiological and behavioral components. An attitude is a lasting evaluation of something, made up of affect, behavior and cognition.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'The three components of emotion are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['cognitive / physiological / genetic','physiological / behavioral / motivational','cognitive / physiological / behavioral','cognitive / social / behavioral'],ans:2,exp:'Emotion has a **cognitive** (subjective appraisal), a **physiological** (autonomic arousal) and a **behavioral** (facial expression, action) component. Theories of emotion differ mainly in the order they assign to these components.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'The 7 universal emotions are _____, _____, _____, _____, _____, _____, and _____',choices:['happiness / sadness / fear / disgust / anger / surprise / love','happiness / sadness / fear / guilt / anger / surprise / contempt','happiness / sadness / fear / disgust / anger / surprise / contempt','happiness / sadness / fear / disgust / anger / pride / contempt'],ans:2,exp:'Paul Ekman identified 7 emotions with facial expressions recognized across cultures: **happiness, sadness, fear, disgust, anger, surprise and contempt**. Love, guilt and pride are not on the list because their expressions are not universal.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'The _____ states that behavioral and physiological responses lead to emotions',choices:['James-Lange theory','Cannon-Bard theory','Schachter-Singer theory','opponent-process theory'],ans:0,exp:'The **James-Lange theory** says we feel afraid because we tremble: arousal comes first, then emotion. Patients with spinal cord injuries who still feel emotions are evidence against it.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'The _____ states that emotional and physiological responses to a stimulus occur simultaneously',choices:['James-Lange theory','Schachter-Singer theory','Cannon-Bard theory','opponent-process theory'],ans:2,exp:'In the **Cannon-Bard theory**, the thalamus sends signals to the cortex (emotion) and the autonomic system (arousal) at the same time and independently. In the James-Lange theory, arousal comes first.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'The _____ states that physiological arousal plus a cognitive label based on context leads to emotion',choices:['James-Lange theory','Cannon-Bard theory','Schachter-Singer theory','opponent-process theory'],ans:2,exp:'The **Schachter-Singer (two-factor) theory** says arousal is interpreted through situational cues; in their epinephrine study, the same arousal was labeled euphoria or anger depending on the people around. Only this theory requires a cognitive label.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'_____ is the physiological and cognitive response to challenges',choices:['Emotion','Motivation','Arousal','Stress'],ans:3,exp:'**Stress** involves appraising a challenge and activating the HPA axis and the sympathetic nervous system. Arousal is part of the stress response but does not by itself involve evaluating a challenge.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'_____ is the process by which individuals evaluate and cope with a stressful event',choices:['General adaptation syndrome','Stress appraisal','Eustress','Learned helplessness'],ans:1,exp:'**Stress appraisal** has two steps: primary appraisal (is this a threat?) and secondary appraisal (can I cope with it?). General adaptation syndrome describes the body\'s physical stress response, not this evaluation.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'A _____ is anything that leads to a stress response',choices:['stress appraisal','coping mechanism','defense mechanism','stressor'],ans:3,exp:'**Stressors** can be environmental (noise), daily hassles, major life changes (marriage, job loss) or social. Both positive and negative changes can be stressors.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'Eustress is _____ and distress is _____',choices:['bad stress / good stress','acute stress / chronic stress','good stress / bad stress','chronic stress / acute stress'],ans:2,exp:'**Eustress** ("eu" = good) is positive, motivating stress, such as a promotion or a wedding. **Distress** is unpleasant stress that can harm health; the difference is whether stress is positive or negative, not how long it lasts.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'_____ explains that stressors do not produce specific responses; they all generate the same general physical stress response',choices:['Stress appraisal','Opponent-process theory','General adaptation syndrome','The fight-or-flight response'],ans:2,exp:'Hans Selye\'s **general adaptation syndrome** describes a nonspecific bodily reaction to any stressor that unfolds in three stages. Stress appraisal is the cognitive evaluation of a stressor, not the physical response.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'6C',q:'In order, the three stages of the general adaptation syndrome are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['alarm / resistance / exhaustion','resistance / alarm / exhaustion','alarm / exhaustion / resistance','alarm / appraisal / recovery'],ans:0,exp:'In **alarm**, sympathetic activation and cortisol rise; in **resistance**, the body sustains its effort against the stressor; in **exhaustion**, its resources are depleted, raising the risk of illness. Mnemonic: ARE.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is the sum of the ways we describe ourselves',choices:['Self-esteem','Self-efficacy','Locus of control','Self-concept'],ans:3,exp:'**Self-concept** is our overall picture of who we are, past, present and future (possible selves). Self-esteem is how we evaluate that picture.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ are the individual components of our self-concept related to the groups to which we belong',choices:['Personality traits','Self-esteem','Self-efficacy','Identities'],ans:3,exp:'**Identities** (e.g., gender, ethnic, religious, occupational) are the parts of the self-concept tied to group membership; a person has many. Self-esteem is a global evaluation of self-worth, not a group-based component.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is an individual\'s sense of their own value or worth',choices:['Self-efficacy','Self-concept','Self-esteem','Locus of control'],ans:2,exp:'**Self-esteem** is the evaluative judgment of our self-concept. Self-efficacy is narrower: belief in one\'s ability to succeed at a specific task.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is the degree to which we see ourselves as capable at a given skill or in a given situation',choices:['Self-esteem','Locus of control','Self-efficacy','Self-concept'],ans:2,exp:'**Self-efficacy** (Bandura) is task-specific and predicts persistence; a person can have high self-efficacy in math but low self-efficacy in sports. Self-esteem is global self-worth.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is a sense of powerlessness arising from a traumatic event or from persistent failure to succeed',choices:['Learned helplessness','Self-handicapping','Cognitive dissonance','Impostor syndrome'],ans:0,exp:'**Learned helplessness** (Seligman) was shown in dogs that stopped trying to escape shocks after earlier shocks were unavoidable. It is a model for depression.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is the degree to which people believe they control the outcomes of events in their lives, as opposed to external forces',choices:['Self-efficacy','Self-esteem','Locus of control','Learned helplessness'],ans:2,exp:'An internal **locus of control** attributes outcomes to one\'s own effort, while an external one attributes them to luck or others (Rotter). Self-efficacy is belief in one\'s ability at a task, not about who controls outcomes.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s psychosexual stages of personality development are based on tensions caused by the _____',choices:['superego','libido','persona','reality principle'],ans:1,exp:'Freud held that the **libido** (sexual energy) focuses on a different body zone at each stage, and unresolved conflict causes **fixation**. The superego is the moral part of personality, not the energy source.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s oral psychosexual stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['0 / 1','1 / 3','3 / 6','0 / 2'],ans:0,exp:'In the **oral stage (0–1)**, pleasure centers on the mouth (sucking, feeding). Freud linked fixation here to dependency or oral habits like smoking. Order: oral, anal, phallic, latent, genital.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s anal psychosexual stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['0 / 1','1 / 3','3 / 6','2 / 4'],ans:1,exp:'The **anal stage (1–3)** centers on toilet training, and fixation produces an anal-retentive (overly orderly) or anal-expulsive (messy) personality. It overlaps in age with Erikson\'s autonomy vs. shame and doubt stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s phallic psychosexual stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['1 / 3','6 / 12','3 / 6','0 / 1'],ans:2,exp:'In the **phallic stage (3–6)**, the **Oedipus** (and Electra) conflict is resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. The latent stage (6–puberty) follows.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s latent psychosexual stage lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['3 / 6','1 / 3','12 / 20','6 / 12'],ans:3,exp:'In the **latent stage (6 to puberty, about 12)**, sexual impulses are dormant and children focus on same-sex friendships, school and skills. The genital stage begins at puberty.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Freud\'s genital psychosexual stage lasts from _____ through _____',choices:['puberty / adulthood','birth / age 1','age 6 / puberty','age 3 / age 6'],ans:0,exp:'The **genital stage** begins at **puberty**, when sexual interest matures and is directed toward others. Age 6 to puberty is the latent stage, and age 3 to 6 is the phallic stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Each of Erikson\'s 8 stages of psychosocial development is defined by a _____ that must be resolved',choices:['conflict (crisis)','libidinal fixation','moral dilemma','cognitive schema'],ans:0,exp:'Erikson described a lifelong series of psychosocial **conflicts** (e.g., trust vs. mistrust); resolving each one builds a virtue such as hope or will. Libidinal fixation is Freud\'s concept, and moral dilemmas are Kohlberg\'s.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s trust vs. mistrust stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['1 / 3','3 / 6','0 / 1','6 / 12'],ans:2,exp:'**Trust vs. mistrust (0–1)**: an infant whose needs are met consistently learns that the world is safe, gaining the virtue of hope. It corresponds to Freud\'s oral stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s autonomy vs. shame and doubt stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['0 / 1','3 / 6','6 / 12','1 / 3'],ans:3,exp:'**Autonomy vs. shame and doubt (1–3)**: toddlers gain independence in toileting, feeding and dressing, gaining the virtue of will. It lines up with Freud\'s anal stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s initiative vs. guilt stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['3 / 6','1 / 3','6 / 12','12 / 20'],ans:0,exp:'**Initiative vs. guilt (3–6)**: preschoolers start activities and plan play; if they are overly controlled, they feel guilt. The virtue gained is purpose.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s industry vs. inferiority stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['3 / 6','6 / 12','12 / 20','20 / 40'],ans:1,exp:'**Industry vs. inferiority (6–12)**: school-age children build competence through achievement, gaining the virtue of competence. It parallels Freud\'s latent stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s identity vs. role confusion stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['6 / 12','20 / 40','12 / 20','40 / 65'],ans:2,exp:'**Identity vs. role confusion (12–20)**: adolescents explore roles and values to form a stable sense of self, gaining the virtue of fidelity. Intimacy vs. isolation follows in early adulthood.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s intimacy vs. isolation stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['12 / 20','40 / 65','6 / 12','20 / 40'],ans:3,exp:'**Intimacy vs. isolation (20–40)**: young adults form close, committed relationships, gaining the virtue of love. A secure identity from the previous stage makes intimacy possible.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s generativity vs. stagnation stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ to age _____',choices:['40 / 65','20 / 40','65 / death','12 / 20'],ans:0,exp:'**Generativity vs. stagnation (40–65)**: middle-aged adults contribute to the next generation through parenting, work and mentoring, gaining the virtue of care.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Erikson\'s integrity vs. despair stage of psychosocial development lasts from age _____ until _____',choices:['40 / 65','65 / death','20 / 40','12 / 20'],ans:1,exp:'**Integrity vs. despair (65 to death)**: older adults look back on life with satisfaction or regret, gaining the virtue of wisdom.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ are stages based on responses to moral dilemmas (such as the Heinz dilemma) that explain how children develop moral reasoning',choices:['Piaget\'s stages','Erikson\'s stages','Kohlberg\'s stages','Freud\'s stages'],ans:2,exp:'**Kohlberg** described three levels of moral reasoning (preconventional, conventional, postconventional), each with two stages. Carol Gilligan criticized the model for undervaluing a care-based morality.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Kohlberg\'s preconventional level of moral reasoning is most typical of _____',choices:['adolescents and most adults','young children (before about age 9)','only some adults','infants before language develops'],ans:1,exp:'**Preconventional** reasoning (obedience to avoid punishment, then self-interest) is typical of **young children**. Kohlberg tied his levels to reasoning, not to fixed ages, so age ranges are only approximate.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Kohlberg\'s conventional level of moral reasoning is most typical of _____',choices:['young children (before about age 9)','infants before language develops','adolescents and most adults','only some adults'],ans:2,exp:'**Conventional** reasoning (conformity for approval, then law and order) begins in adolescence and is where **most adults** stay. Only a minority of adults reach postconventional reasoning.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Kohlberg\'s postconventional level of moral reasoning is reached by _____',choices:['only some adults','adolescents and most adults','young children (before about age 9)','infants before language develops'],ans:0,exp:'**Postconventional** reasoning (social contract, then universal ethical principles) is reached by **only some adults**, usually not before late adolescence. Most adults stay at the conventional level.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Kohlberg\'s _____ morality is based on self-interest and a child-like sense of right and wrong',choices:['conventional','preconventional','postconventional','social-contract'],ans:1,exp:'**Preconventional** morality judges actions by their consequences for oneself: avoiding punishment (stage 1) or getting rewards through exchange (stage 2). The social-contract stage belongs to the postconventional level.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'A child who displays Kohlberg\'s _____ morality will care for others and obey rules simply because they exist',choices:['preconventional','postconventional','conventional','social-contract'],ans:2,exp:'**Conventional** morality values approval ("good boy/good girl", stage 3) and maintaining social order (law and order, stage 4). Postconventional reasoning would question an unjust rule rather than obey it.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Someone who displays Kohlberg\'s _____ morality uses basic ethical principles to guide their behavior',choices:['preconventional','conventional','postconventional','law-and-order'],ans:2,exp:'**Postconventional** morality judges laws against abstract principles such as justice and human rights (stages 5–6) and may justify civil disobedience. Law-and-order reasoning is stage 4, at the conventional level.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is the range of skills that an individual can perform with assistance but cannot yet perform independently',choices:['Bandura\'s observational learning','Vygotsky\'s zone of proximal development','Piaget\'s concrete operational stage','Kohlberg\'s conventional morality'],ans:1,exp:'Vygotsky\'s **zone of proximal development** is the gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can do with help (**scaffolding**) from a more knowledgeable other. Piaget\'s stages describe what a child can do independently at each age.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'Children often play with each other using _____ and _____',choices:['competition / isolation','imitation / role-taking','punishment / reward','assimilation / accommodation'],ans:1,exp:'Through **imitation** and **role-taking** (e.g., playing house), children learn social roles and how others see them, which corresponds to Mead\'s play stage. Assimilation and accommodation are Piaget\'s cognitive processes, not forms of play.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'8A',q:'A reference group is the group to which we _____',choices:['belong by birth','feel hostility toward','compare ourselves','have no contact with'],ans:2,exp:'A **reference group** is a standard we **compare ourselves** to when judging our attitudes, behavior and success; we do not need to belong to it. A group we feel hostility toward is an out-group.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ perspective of personality states that personality results from unconscious urges and desires',choices:['humanistic','behaviorist','trait','psychoanalytic'],ans:3,exp:'The **psychoanalytic** perspective (Freud, Jung, Adler, Horney) emphasizes unconscious conflict and early childhood. The humanistic perspective focuses on conscious growth and free will instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'In Freud\'s theory, personality is formed through conflicts among three fundamental structures of the mind: the _____, the _____, and the _____',choices:['id / ego / preconscious','conscious / preconscious / unconscious','id / ego / superego','id / ego / libido'],ans:2,exp:'Freud\'s structural model has the **id**, **ego** and **superego**. Conscious, preconscious and unconscious belong to his separate topographic model of levels of awareness.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'In Freud\'s theory of personality, the _____ is in charge of our base urges of survival and reproduction',choices:['ego','superego','id','ego ideal'],ans:2,exp:'The **id** is entirely unconscious and runs on the **pleasure principle**, seeking immediate gratification. The ego works on the reality principle instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'In Freud\'s theory of personality, the _____ is the idealist and perfectionist',choices:['id','superego','ego','pleasure principle'],ans:1,exp:'The **superego** holds internalized moral standards (the conscience and the ego ideal) and causes guilt when they are broken. It develops when the phallic-stage conflict is resolved.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'In Freud\'s theory of personality, the _____ is the mediator between the _____, the _____, and the conscious mind',choices:['id / ego / superego','ego / id / superego','superego / id / ego','ego / unconscious / preconscious'],ans:1,exp:'The **ego** follows the **reality principle**, balancing the id\'s urges against the superego\'s morals and the demands of reality, and it uses defense mechanisms to reduce anxiety.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Jung\'s theory of the collective unconscious states that personality is influenced by _____',choices:['inferiority feelings','archetypes','libidinal fixations','conditioned responses'],ans:1,exp:'Jung proposed inherited, universal **archetypes** (e.g., the persona, the shadow, the anima and animus) shared by all humans. Inferiority feelings belong to Adler\'s theory.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Adler and Horney\'s theories of personality state that the unconscious is motivated mainly by _____',choices:['sexual urges','archetypes','social urges','conditioned reinforcement'],ans:2,exp:'The neo-Freudians **Adler** (inferiority complex, striving for superiority) and **Horney** (basic anxiety in relationships) put **social** motives ahead of Freud\'s sexual drives.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ perspective of personality emphasizes the internal feelings of healthy individuals as they strive for happiness and self-realization',choices:['psychoanalytic','behaviorist','humanistic','biological'],ans:2,exp:'The **humanistic** perspective (Maslow, Rogers) stresses free will, personal growth and self-actualization. The psychoanalytic view emphasizes unconscious conflict instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Carl Rogers\' _____ involves showing complete support and acceptance of a person no matter what that person says or does',choices:['conditions of worth','unconditional positive regard','self-actualization','empathic reflection'],ans:1,exp:'**Unconditional positive regard** is central to Rogers\' client-centered therapy because it helps align the real self with the ideal self. Conditions of worth, where acceptance depends on behavior, do the opposite.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ states that personality can be described by identifiable traits that carry characteristic behaviors',choices:['Psychoanalytic theory','Social cognitive theory','Humanistic theory','Type and trait theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Type and trait theories** (Eysenck, Allport, the Big Five) describe personality with measurable dimensions or categories rather than explaining where it comes from. Psychoanalytic theory explains personality through unconscious conflict instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is the belief that an individual\'s personality can be sorted into a few distinct categories',choices:['Trait theory','Type theory','Biological theory','Social cognitive theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Type theory** sorts people into categories (e.g., Type A vs. Type B, or Hippocrates\' four humors). Trait theory instead places people along continuous dimensions.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Hans Eysenck\'s three dimensions of personality are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['openness / extraversion / neuroticism','psychoticism / extraversion / agreeableness','cardinal / central / secondary','psychoticism / extraversion / neuroticism'],ans:3,exp:'Eysenck\'s **PEN model** is psychoticism (nonconformity, aggression), extraversion and neuroticism (emotional instability). Openness and agreeableness belong to the Big Five, and cardinal, central and secondary are Allport\'s traits.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The Big Five personality traits are _____, _____, _____, _____, and _____',choices:['openness / conscientiousness / extraversion / agreeableness / psychoticism','openness / conscientiousness / extraversion / agreeableness / neuroticism','optimism / conscientiousness / extraversion / agreeableness / neuroticism','openness / conscientiousness / extraversion / altruism / neuroticism'],ans:1,exp:'Mnemonic **OCEAN**: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism. Psychoticism belongs to Eysenck\'s PEN model, not the Big Five.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Gordon Allport\'s trait theory includes three basic kinds of traits: _____, _____, and _____',choices:['cardinal / central / peripheral','cardinal / central / secondary','primary / secondary / tertiary','source / surface / central'],ans:1,exp:'Allport\'s **cardinal** traits dominate a whole personality (few people have one), **central** traits are the main characteristics, and **secondary** traits appear only in certain situations. Source and surface traits come from Cattell.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ of personality states that individuals interact with their environment in a cycle called reciprocal determinism',choices:['behaviorist perspective','humanistic perspective','biological perspective','social cognitive perspective'],ans:3,exp:'Bandura\'s **social cognitive perspective** says thoughts, behavior and environment all influence each other (**reciprocal determinism**). Pure behaviorism ignores the role of cognition.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ of personality states that our personality develops as a result of operant conditioning',choices:['social cognitive perspective','humanistic perspective','behaviorist perspective','psychoanalytic perspective'],ans:2,exp:'In the **behaviorist perspective** (Skinner), personality is the set of behaviors shaped by reinforcement and punishment. The social cognitive perspective adds thoughts and observational learning to this.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ of personality states that behavior can be explained as a result of genetic expression',choices:['behaviorist perspective','biological perspective','humanistic perspective','social cognitive perspective'],ans:1,exp:'The **biological perspective** draws on twin and adoption studies showing that traits such as temperament are substantially heritable. The behaviorist perspective explains behavior through environmental conditioning instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is characterized by thoughts or experiences that seem out of touch with reality, disorganized speech or behavior, and decreased participation in daily activities',choices:['Bipolar I disorder','Major depressive disorder','Schizophrenia','Dissociative identity disorder'],ans:2,exp:'In DSM-5-TR, **schizophrenia** requires at least 2 symptoms (at least one of them delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech) and continuous signs for at least 6 months. Dissociative identity disorder involves separate identity states, not psychosis.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ symptoms of schizophrenia add something to behavior, cognition, or affect, such as delusions or hallucinations',choices:['Negative','Positive','Prodromal','Residual'],ans:1,exp:'**Positive** symptoms are added experiences or behaviors: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech. Negative symptoms are losses such as flat affect, avolition and alogia.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are characterized by sadness severe enough or persistent enough to interfere with function',choices:['Anxiety disorders','Bipolar and related disorders','Depressive disorders','Somatic symptom disorders'],ans:2,exp:'**Depressive disorders** include major depressive disorder, persistent depressive disorder and premenstrual dysphoric disorder. In DSM-5-TR, bipolar disorders are a separate chapter because they involve mania or hypomania.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is a depressed mood present most of the day, on more days than not, for at least _____ years in adults',choices:['1','2','5','10'],ans:1,exp:'**Persistent depressive disorder** requires at least **2 years** of depressed mood in adults (1 year in children and adolescents). DSM-5 merged dysthymia with chronic major depression, so major depressive episodes can also occur during that time.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is a form of depression related to changes in season, classified in DSM-5-TR as major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern',choices:['Cyclothymic disorder','Persistent depressive disorder','Seasonal affective disorder','Premenstrual dysphoric disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Seasonal affective disorder** usually appears in fall or winter as daylight shortens and is treated with bright-light therapy. In DSM-5-TR it is a specifier ("with seasonal pattern"), not a separate disorder.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ causes extreme mood swings that include emotional highs (mania or hypomania) and lows (depression)',choices:['Major depressive disorder','Persistent depressive disorder','Borderline personality disorder','Bipolar disorder'],ans:3,exp:'**Bipolar disorder** involves distinct episodes of mania or hypomania, usually alternating with depression; lithium is a classic treatment. Borderline personality disorder causes rapid emotional instability but not true manic episodes.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ disorder requires at least one manic episode, which may be preceded or followed by hypomanic or major depressive episodes',choices:['Bipolar I','Bipolar II','Cyclothymic','Persistent depressive'],ans:0,exp:'**Bipolar I** needs just one full **manic** episode (at least 1 week, or any duration if hospitalized); a depressive episode is not required. Bipolar II involves hypomania and never full mania.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ disorder requires at least one major depressive episode and at least one hypomanic episode, but no manic episode',choices:['Bipolar I','Bipolar II','Cyclothymic','Persistent depressive'],ans:1,exp:'**Bipolar II** combines **hypomania** (at least 4 days, without marked impairment) with major depression. Any full manic episode changes the diagnosis to bipolar I.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ involves at least 2 years (1 year in children) of numerous periods of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet the criteria for a hypomanic or major depressive episode',choices:['Bipolar I disorder','Bipolar II disorder','Cyclothymic disorder','Persistent depressive disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Cyclothymic disorder** is chronic, milder mood cycling that falls below the thresholds for full episodes. Persistent depressive disorder has no hypomanic symptoms.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are disorders that involve excessive fear or worry',choices:['Depressive disorders','Dissociative disorders','Anxiety disorders','Somatic symptom disorders'],ans:2,exp:'**Anxiety disorders** include generalized anxiety disorder, panic disorder, specific phobia and social anxiety disorder. In DSM-5-TR, OCD and PTSD have their own chapters.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is characterized by excessive, intrusive thoughts that lead to repetitive behaviors',choices:['Generalized anxiety disorder','Panic disorder','Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder','Obsessive-compulsive disorder'],ans:3,exp:'In **OCD**, intrusive **obsessions** cause anxiety that is relieved by **compulsions** (e.g., hand-washing), and the person recognizes these as unwanted. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder involves rigid perfectionism without true obsessions or compulsions.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is characterized by an unrealistically negative evaluation of one\'s appearance and preoccupation with perceived physical defects',choices:['Illness anxiety disorder','Social anxiety disorder','Body dysmorphic disorder','Somatic symptom disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Body dysmorphic disorder** is grouped with OCD-related disorders in DSM-5-TR and involves repetitive behaviors such as mirror-checking. Illness anxiety disorder is about fear of disease, not appearance.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'If a person has persistent difficulty recovering (for more than a month) after experiencing or witnessing a terrifying event, they may develop _____',choices:['generalized anxiety disorder','panic disorder','obsessive-compulsive disorder','posttraumatic stress disorder'],ans:3,exp:'**PTSD** involves intrusive memories, avoidance, negative changes in mood and thinking, and hyperarousal lasting more than 1 month. DSM-5-TR places it with the trauma- and stressor-related disorders; GAD does not require a traumatic event.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are disorders that involve a disconnection and lack of continuity between thoughts, memories, surroundings, actions, and identity',choices:['Somatic symptom disorders','Psychotic disorders','Anxiety disorders','Dissociative disorders'],ans:3,exp:'**Dissociative disorders** include dissociative amnesia, dissociative identity disorder and depersonalization/derealization disorder, and they are often linked to trauma. Unlike psychotic disorders, reality testing usually stays intact.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'A person with _____ cannot recall important autobiographical information, usually about a traumatic or stressful event, without a medical cause',choices:['dissociative identity disorder','depersonalization/derealization disorder','dissociative amnesia','conversion disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Dissociative amnesia** is memory loss of psychological rather than neurological origin and may occur with dissociative fugue. Depersonalization/derealization disorder involves feeling detached, with memory intact.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'In dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, a person loses awareness of their _____ or other important autobiographical information and engages in some form of _____',choices:['identity / unexpected travel or wandering','identity / compulsive rituals','surroundings / hallucinations','body / unexplained paralysis'],ans:0,exp:'**Dissociative fugue** combines amnesia for **identity** with purposeful **travel or wandering**, sometimes with a new identity. In DSM-5-TR it is a specifier of dissociative amnesia, not a separate disorder.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Dissociative identity disorder is characterized by _____',choices:['hallucinations and delusions','detachment from one\'s own body','rapid cycling between mania and depression','two or more distinct personality states'],ans:3,exp:'**Dissociative identity disorder** (formerly multiple personality disorder) involves **two or more distinct personality states** with gaps in memory, and it is strongly linked to childhood trauma. It is not schizophrenia, which involves psychosis rather than separate identities.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Someone with depersonalization/derealization disorder experiences a feeling of detachment from the _____ or the _____',choices:['memories / identity','mind and body / surroundings','emotions / relationships','past / future'],ans:1,exp:'**Depersonalization** is detachment from one\'s own **mind and body** (feeling like an outside observer), and **derealization** is detachment from the **surroundings** (the world feels unreal). Reality testing remains intact.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are characterized by physical symptoms that cause significant distress and are often associated with psychological stress',choices:['Dissociative disorders','Anxiety disorders','Depressive disorders','Somatic symptom and related disorders'],ans:3,exp:'**Somatic symptom and related disorders** include somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder and conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder). The symptoms are not intentionally produced, which separates them from malingering.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is when a person has excessive, distressing thoughts and anxiety about physical symptoms that are actually present, such as pain or fatigue',choices:['Illness anxiety disorder','Conversion disorder','Somatic symptom disorder','Body dysmorphic disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Somatic symptom disorder** requires at least one distressing physical symptom plus excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviors about it, whether or not a medical cause exists. Illness anxiety disorder involves few or no symptoms.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ is a preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious, undiagnosed illness, with few or no physical symptoms',choices:['Somatic symptom disorder','Conversion disorder','Illness anxiety disorder','Body dysmorphic disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Illness anxiety disorder** (formerly hypochondriasis) centers on fear of disease, not on actual symptoms. Somatic symptom disorder is defined by real, distressing symptoms instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ (functional neurological symptom disorder) involves neurological symptoms such as paralysis or blindness that are incompatible with any recognized medical condition',choices:['Somatic symptom disorder','Illness anxiety disorder','Conversion disorder','Body dysmorphic disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Conversion disorder** produces a real loss of motor or sensory function, often after stress; some patients show "la belle indifférence" (lack of concern). Unlike factitious disorder, the symptoms are not faked.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are a group of disorders involving a rigid and unhealthy pattern of thinking, functioning, and behaving',choices:['Mood disorders','Anxiety disorders','Psychotic disorders','Personality disorders'],ans:3,exp:'**Personality disorders** are long-lasting, inflexible patterns that begin by adolescence or early adulthood, and they are usually **ego-syntonic** (the person sees them as normal). Mood disorders are episodic rather than lifelong patterns.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Cluster A of the personality disorders, the odd or eccentric ("weird") cluster, includes _____',choices:['paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal','antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic','avoidant, dependent, and obsessive-compulsive','schizophrenia, delusional, and schizoaffective'],ans:0,exp:'**Cluster A** is **paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal**, linked to a family history of schizophrenia. Schizophrenia, delusional and schizoaffective disorder are psychotic disorders, not personality disorders.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Cluster B of the personality disorders, the dramatic, emotional, or erratic ("wild") cluster, includes _____',choices:['paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal','antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic','avoidant, dependent, and obsessive-compulsive','schizophrenia, delusional, and schizoaffective'],ans:1,exp:'**Cluster B** is **antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic**. Antisocial personality disorder requires age 18 or older and evidence of conduct disorder before age 15.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Cluster C of the personality disorders, the anxious or fearful ("worried") cluster, includes _____',choices:['paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal','antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic','avoidant, dependent, and obsessive-compulsive','schizophrenia, delusional, and schizoaffective'],ans:2,exp:'**Cluster C** is **avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive** personality disorder. Mnemonic for the clusters: A = weird, B = wild, C = worried.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ to psychological disorders focuses on how environmental factors affect observable behavior',choices:['biomedical approach','biopsychosocial approach','psychodynamic approach','behaviorist approach'],ans:3,exp:'The **behaviorist approach** treats disorders as learned behaviors and uses conditioning-based treatments such as systematic desensitization and exposure. The psychodynamic approach looks for unconscious conflict instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ to psychological disorders focuses on physical causes and symptoms',choices:['biopsychosocial approach','behaviorist approach','psychodynamic approach','biomedical approach'],ans:3,exp:'The **biomedical approach** looks for physical causes (genes, neurotransmitters, brain lesions) and treats them with medication or surgery. Its weakness is ignoring psychological and social factors, which the biopsychosocial approach adds.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ to psychological disorders considers the relative contributions of biological, psychological, and social components',choices:['biopsychosocial approach','biomedical approach','behaviorist approach','psychodynamic approach'],ans:0,exp:'The **biopsychosocial approach** (Engel) is the dominant modern model of mental illness and of health in general. The biomedical approach looks only at physical causes.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ to psychological disorders states that human functioning is based on the interaction of drives and forces within the person, particularly the unconscious',choices:['behaviorist approach','biomedical approach','psychodynamic approach','biopsychosocial approach'],ans:2,exp:'The **psychodynamic approach** comes from Freud and traces symptoms to unconscious conflict and childhood experience. The behaviorist approach rejects unobservable inner forces altogether.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5-TR) categorizes mental disorders based on _____',choices:['underlying causes','symptoms','theoretical orientation','response to treatment'],ans:1,exp:'The **DSM** is descriptive and atheoretical: it classifies disorders by their **symptoms** (diagnostic criteria), not by presumed causes. DSM-5-TR (2022) is the current edition.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Biological risk factors for _____ include genetic factors, birth trauma, marijuana use, and family history',choices:['bipolar disorder','major depressive disorder','schizophrenia','generalized anxiety disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Schizophrenia** is strongly heritable and is linked to obstetric complications and heavy adolescent cannabis use. It is associated with excess dopamine activity, enlarged ventricles and **hypofrontality**.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Biological factors related to _____ include high glucocorticoids, low norepinephrine, low serotonin, and low dopamine',choices:['schizophrenia','bipolar disorder','depression','Alzheimer\'s disease'],ans:2,exp:'**Depression** is linked to HPA-axis overactivity (high cortisol) and to low monoamines, which is the rationale for SSRIs and SNRIs. Bipolar disorder is linked to high norepinephrine instead, at least during mania.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Biological factors related to _____ include high norepinephrine, abnormal serotonin, and a strong family history',choices:['major depressive disorder','schizophrenia','generalized anxiety disorder','bipolar disorders'],ans:3,exp:'**Bipolar disorders** are among the most heritable psychiatric conditions, and mania is linked to excess norepinephrine. Depression, by contrast, is linked to low norepinephrine.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Biological factors related to _____ include genetic factors, brain atrophy, low acetylcholine, and senile plaques of β-amyloid',choices:['Parkinson\'s disease','Huntington\'s disease','Korsakoff syndrome','Alzheimer\'s disease'],ans:3,exp:'**Alzheimer\'s disease** shows **β-amyloid plaques**, **tau neurofibrillary tangles** and loss of cholinergic neurons, with early hippocampal atrophy; the APOE ε4 allele raises risk. Parkinson\'s disease involves dopamine loss instead.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'Signs and biological findings of _____ include bradykinesia, resting (pill-rolling) tremor, masklike facies, cogwheel rigidity, shuffling gait, and low dopamine',choices:['Alzheimer\'s disease','Huntington\'s disease','Parkinson\'s disease','Korsakoff syndrome'],ans:2,exp:'**Parkinson\'s disease** results from loss of dopaminergic neurons in the **substantia nigra** (nigrostriatal pathway) and is treated with L-DOPA. Huntington\'s disease causes involuntary jerky movements (chorea) rather than slowness and tremor.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ describes the tendency of people to perform at a different level when others are around',choices:['Social loafing','Social facilitation','Deindividuation','Group polarization'],ans:1,exp:'In **social facilitation**, the presence of others raises arousal, which improves performance on simple or well-practiced tasks and worsens it on complex or new ones. Social loafing is reduced effort when individual contributions are hidden in a group.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is a loss of self-awareness and individual identity in large groups',choices:['Deindividuation','Social loafing','Social facilitation','Group polarization'],ans:0,exp:'**Deindividuation** is driven by anonymity and arousal, and it can lead to antisocial acts such as riots or online harassment. Group polarization is about group decisions becoming more extreme, not loss of identity.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'According to the _____, individuals in a group are less likely to help a person in need than individuals who are alone',choices:['bystander effect','social loafing','social facilitation','deindividuation'],ans:0,exp:'The **bystander effect** (e.g., the Kitty Genovese case) is driven by **diffusion of responsibility** and by people looking to others to decide whether help is needed. More bystanders means each person is less likely to help.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the social influence placed on individuals by others they consider equals',choices:['Obedience','Peer pressure','Group polarization','Deindividuation'],ans:1,exp:'**Peer pressure** comes from equals and is strongest in adolescence. Obedience is the response to an authority figure rather than to equals.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is when an individual does not pull their weight in a group setting',choices:['Social loafing','Social facilitation','Deindividuation','Groupthink'],ans:0,exp:'**Social loafing** happens when individual effort cannot be identified (e.g., group tug-of-war or group projects). Making each person accountable reduces it.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen members\' initial leanings, producing more extreme decisions',choices:['Groupthink','Group polarization','Social facilitation','Social loafing'],ans:1,exp:'**Group polarization** pushes the group\'s view further in the direction it already leaned, whether risky or cautious. Groupthink is a different failure: suppressing dissent to keep harmony.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the tendency for groups to make decisions based on ideas and solutions that arise within the group without considering outside ideas',choices:['Group polarization','Social loafing','Groupthink','Deindividuation'],ans:2,exp:'**Groupthink** (Janis) occurs in cohesive groups that value agreement over critical evaluation, with illusions of invulnerability and self-censorship; the Bay of Pigs invasion is the classic example. Group polarization is about decisions becoming more extreme, not about ignoring outside ideas.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'_____ is a term used to describe the beliefs, ideas, behaviors, actions, and characteristics of a group or society',choices:['Society','Culture','Socialization','Subculture'],ans:1,exp:'**Culture** has material parts (physical objects) and symbolic or nonmaterial parts (values, beliefs, language). A society is the group of people who share a culture, not the culture itself.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'_____ is the process by which an immigrant or minority group takes up elements of the mainstream culture',choices:['Multiculturalism','Amalgamation','Assimilation','Cultural diffusion'],ans:2,exp:'In **assimilation**, the minority group\'s culture gradually blends into the dominant culture (e.g., through language, intermarriage and residence). In amalgamation, majority and minority cultures combine to form a new culture.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'_____ is the encouragement of multiple cultures within a community to enhance diversity',choices:['Assimilation','Amalgamation','Cultural diffusion','Multiculturalism'],ans:3,exp:'**Multiculturalism** (pluralism) celebrates differences and is often described as a "mosaic" or "salad bowl", in contrast to the "melting pot" of assimilation.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'A _____ is a group of people within a culture who distinguish themselves from the primary culture',choices:['primary group','reference group','subculture','network'],ans:2,exp:'A **subculture** shares the main culture but has its own distinct norms or practices (e.g., goths, gamers). A subculture that actively opposes the dominant culture is called a counterculture.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ are tendencies to express positive or negative feelings or evaluations of something',choices:['Emotions','Norms','Stereotypes','Attitudes'],ans:3,exp:'**Attitudes** have three components (**ABC**): affective, behavioral and cognitive. Emotions are short-lived feeling states, while attitudes are lasting evaluations of a specific target.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'The _____ suggests that beliefs and attitudes serve various psychological functions',choices:['elaboration likelihood model','functional attitudes theory','social cognitive theory','cognitive dissonance theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Functional attitudes theory** says attitudes serve knowledge, ego-expressive, adaptive and ego-defensive functions. The elaboration likelihood model instead explains how attitudes change through persuasion.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7C',q:'The _____ states that behavior is learned by watching and imitating others',choices:['functional attitudes theory','elaboration likelihood model','cognitive dissonance theory','observational learning theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Observational learning theory** (Bandura) involves attention, retention, reproduction and motivation, and it relies on **modeling**. Cognitive dissonance theory instead explains attitude change caused by inconsistency between beliefs and actions.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7C',q:'The _____ describes how people are persuaded to change their attitudes',choices:['functional attitudes theory','social cognitive theory','elaboration likelihood model','observational learning theory'],ans:2,exp:'The **elaboration likelihood model** has two routes: the **central route** (careful thought about message quality) produces lasting change, and the **peripheral route** (superficial cues such as attractiveness or expertise) produces weaker change.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7C',q:'The _____ states that attitudes are formed through watching others, personal factors, and the environment',choices:['functional attitudes theory','elaboration likelihood model','cognitive dissonance theory','social cognitive theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Social cognitive theory** (Bandura) applies **reciprocal determinism**: behavior, personal factors (thoughts) and environment influence one another. The elaboration likelihood model is about persuasion, not attitude formation through observation.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the process of internalizing the social norms and values expected in one\'s society',choices:['Assimilation','Conformity','Socialization','Deviance'],ans:2,exp:'**Socialization** is carried out by agents such as family, school, peers and media, and it continues throughout life. Conformity is changing behavior to match a group in a specific situation, not lifelong internalization.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ are a method of enforcing compliance with social norms',choices:['Norms','Mores','Taboos','Sanctions'],ans:3,exp:'**Sanctions** are rewards or penalties for following or breaking norms; they can be formal (laws, fines) or informal (praise, a dirty look). Norms are the rules themselves, and sanctions enforce them.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'Positive sanctions are a _____ for good behavior; negative sanctions are a _____ for bad behavior',choices:['punishment / reward','reward / punishment','norm / more','reward / reinforcement'],ans:1,exp:'Positive sanctions **reward** conformity (a promotion, applause), and negative sanctions **punish** deviance (a fine, ostracism). "Positive" and "negative" here mean approval versus disapproval, not adding versus removing as in operant conditioning.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'A(n) _____ is a sanction that is not enforced by an authority but occurs in everyday interactions with other people',choices:['formal sanction','law','official policy','informal sanction'],ans:3,exp:'**Informal sanctions** (e.g., a compliment, an eye-roll, being shunned) come from peers in daily life. Formal sanctions are imposed by institutions such as courts or employers.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ determine the boundaries of acceptable behavior within a society',choices:['Values','Roles','Statuses','Norms'],ans:3,exp:'**Norms** are the rules of behavior and range from folkways to mores to taboos to laws. Values are broader beliefs about what is good or important, and norms put those values into practice.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ are informal norms of major importance to society that, if broken, can result in severe sanctions',choices:['Folkways','Laws','Values','Mores'],ans:3,exp:'**Mores** carry moral weight (e.g., not lying, not cheating on a spouse). Laws are formal, written norms, and folkways are minor norms of etiquette.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ are informal norms that are less significant but still shape our everyday behavior',choices:['Mores','Laws','Values','Folkways'],ans:3,exp:'**Folkways** are everyday conventions (e.g., shaking hands, not cutting in line), and breaking them brings only mild disapproval. Mores carry moral weight and much stronger sanctions.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'Mores govern what we think of as _____ vs. _____',choices:['right / rude','polite / rude','legal / illegal','right / wrong'],ans:3,exp:'**Mores** separate morally **right** from **wrong**, while folkways separate right from rude. Legal versus illegal is the domain of laws, which are formal norms.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'Folkways govern what we think of as _____ vs. _____',choices:['right / wrong','right / rude','legal / illegal','good / evil'],ans:1,exp:'**Folkways** concern manners: **right** (appropriate) versus **rude**. Questions of right versus wrong belong to mores.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'A _____ is a norm so strong that violating it often brings revulsion',choices:['folkway','law','value','taboo'],ans:3,exp:'A **taboo** (e.g., incest, cannibalism) is the strongest kind of norm, and breaking it provokes disgust. Folkways bring only mild disapproval when broken.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the extreme disapproval or dislike of a person or group based on perceived differences from the rest of society',choices:['Deviance','Labeling','Anomie','Stigma'],ans:3,exp:'**Stigma** (Goffman) marks people as discredited because of a trait or condition (e.g., mental illness), and it can become part of their self-concept. Deviance is the act of breaking norms, not the disapproval that follows.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is the violation of norms, rules, or expectations in a society',choices:['Stigma','Anomie','Conformity','Deviance'],ans:3,exp:'**Deviance** is relative to the group\'s norms and is explained by differential association, labeling and strain theories. Anomie is a breakdown of the norms themselves (normlessness), not their violation.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ states that deviance can be learned through our interactions with others',choices:['Labeling theory','Strain theory','Social control theory','Differential association theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Differential association theory** (Sutherland) says deviance is learned from close contacts who favor norm-breaking. Labeling theory instead says that being labeled deviant pushes people into further deviance.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is when someone changes their beliefs or behaviors to fit into a group or society',choices:['Compliance','Obedience','Groupthink','Conformity'],ans:3,exp:'**Conformity** was shown in Asch\'s line experiments, where participants matched an obviously wrong group answer. Compliance responds to a direct request, and obedience responds to a command from authority.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is when someone changes their behavior because of a direct request from others',choices:['Conformity','Obedience','Peer pressure','Compliance'],ans:3,exp:'**Compliance** techniques include foot-in-the-door (a small request first), door-in-the-face (a large request first) and lowball. Obedience is different because it follows a command from authority rather than a request.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7B',q:'_____ is a change in behavior based on a command from someone seen as an authority figure',choices:['Conformity','Obedience','Compliance','Peer pressure'],ans:1,exp:'**Obedience** was shown in Milgram\'s shock experiments, where most participants delivered the maximum shock when an authority figure told them to. Compliance responds to a request from someone without authority.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ describes the position a person occupies in a particular setting',choices:['Role','Norm','Network','Status'],ans:3,exp:'A **status** is a social position (e.g., student, mother), and a role is the set of behaviors expected of that status. Mnemonic: you occupy a status and you play a role.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a position in a social group that one is born into or has no control over',choices:['Achieved status','Master status','Role set','Ascribed status'],ans:3,exp:'**Ascribed statuses** include race, sex and age. Achieved statuses are earned (e.g., physician), and a master status is the one that dominates how others see a person.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a position in a social group that one earns based on merit or one\'s choices',choices:['Ascribed status','Master status','Role set','Achieved status'],ans:3,exp:'**Achieved status** comes from effort or choice (e.g., an Olympic athlete or a lawyer). Ascribed status is assigned at birth or without choice.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the term used to describe the thoughts and behavior expected of someone occupying a status',choices:['Status','Norm','Role set','Role'],ans:3,exp:'A **role** is the expected behavior attached to a status; a doctor is expected to diagnose and to keep confidentiality. A role set is all the roles attached to one status.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ refers to carrying out behaviors that are characteristic of a certain role',choices:['Role set','Role conflict','Role strain','Role performance'],ans:3,exp:'**Role performance** is how well a person actually meets the expectations of a role. Role strain and role conflict describe the difficulties that can arise.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A _____ is the set of all roles associated with one status',choices:['role performance','role conflict','role strain','role set'],ans:3,exp:'A **role set** for a professor, for example, includes teacher, researcher, mentor and committee member, all from one status. Role strain is the tension within a single role.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the term used to describe difficulty managing multiple roles',choices:['Role strain','Role set','Role performance','Role conflict'],ans:3,exp:'**Role conflict** occurs between roles, for example a parent\'s duties clashing with an employee\'s overtime demands. Difficulty within a single role is role strain.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the term used to describe difficulty managing just one role',choices:['Role conflict','Role set','Role performance','Role strain'],ans:3,exp:'**Role strain** occurs within one role, for example a student juggling too many classes. Mnemonic: strain = single role, conflict = clash between roles.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A _____ consists of two or more people with similar characteristics who share a sense of unity',choices:['network','organization','bureaucracy','social group'],ans:3,exp:'A **social group** involves shared identity and interaction, unlike an aggregate (people who simply share a place) or a category (people who share a trait). A network is a pattern of ties that may not involve any sense of unity.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A _____ is a self-selected group formed around shared interests',choices:['family group','reference group','secondary group','peer group'],ans:3,exp:'A **peer group** is joined voluntarily by equals with common interests (e.g., friends, a chess club), and it is a major agent of socialization in adolescence. A family group is not self-selected.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'In an affinal kinship, individuals are related by _____',choices:['blood (common ancestry)','marriage','shared religion','a shared household'],ans:1,exp:'**Affinal** kin (spouses, in-laws) are related through **marriage**. Consanguineous kin share common ancestry (blood).'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'In a consanguineous kinship, individuals are related by _____',choices:['marriage','shared religion','a shared household','blood (common ancestry)'],ans:3,exp:'**Consanguineous** ("same blood") kin share **common ancestry**, such as parents, siblings and cousins. Relationships through marriage are affinal.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a group a person identifies with and feels loyal to, in contrast to groups they compete with or oppose',choices:['Out-group','Reference group','Secondary group','In-group'],ans:3,exp:'The **in-group** is "us", and **in-group bias** leads members to favor it. The out-group is "them", the group a person competes with or opposes.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the group you compete with or oppose',choices:['In-group','Reference group','Primary group','Out-group'],ans:3,exp:'The **out-group** is often perceived as all alike (**out-group homogeneity bias**), which feeds stereotyping. In-group favoritism is the other side of the same us-versus-them divide.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8A',q:'_____ is the group you compare yourself to',choices:['In-group','Out-group','Primary group','Reference group'],ans:3,exp:'A **reference group** sets the standard for self-evaluation, and a person does not have to belong to it (e.g., an aspiring doctor comparing themselves to physicians). An in-group is defined by membership and loyalty, not by comparison.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ involves close, long-lasting relationships with strong emotional bonds',choices:['Secondary group','Reference group','Out-group','Primary group'],ans:3,exp:'A **primary group** (Cooley), such as family or close friends, is valued for the relationships themselves. A secondary group is impersonal and goal-oriented.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ involves impersonal, often temporary relationships with weaker bonds overall',choices:['Primary group','Reference group','In-group','Secondary group'],ans:3,exp:'A **secondary group** (e.g., coworkers, classmates on a project) forms around a task or goal and often dissolves when the task is done. Primary groups are built on strong emotional bonds instead.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a type of society that emphasizes community ties, in which personal relationships and families are given the most importance',choices:['Gesellschaft','Bureaucracy','Oligarchy','Gemeinschaft'],ans:3,exp:'Tönnies\' **Gemeinschaft** ("community") is based on kinship and close personal ties, as in a rural village. **Gesellschaft** ("society") is based on impersonal, self-interested exchange, as in a modern city.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A _____ is an observable pattern of social relationships between individuals or groups',choices:['bureaucracy','organization','dyad','network'],ans:3,exp:'A **social network** maps ties between people. Its structure, such as the strength of weak ties, affects the flow of information and opportunities.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A(n) _____ is a group with identifiable membership that engages in certain actions to achieve a common purpose',choices:['aggregate','crowd','category','organization'],ans:3,exp:'A **formal organization** (e.g., a company or a hospital) has defined membership and goals and outlasts its individual members. An aggregate is just people in the same place with no shared purpose.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'A _____ is a rational system of administration, discipline, and control',choices:['oligarchy','democracy','network','bureaucracy'],ans:3,exp:'Weber\'s ideal **bureaucracy** has a division of labor, a hierarchy, written rules, impersonality and merit-based employment. McDonaldization extends bureaucratic rationality to everyday life.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'The _____ states that democratic or bureaucratic systems naturally shift to being ruled by an elite group',choices:['McDonaldization thesis','Matthew effect','Peter principle','iron law of oligarchy'],ans:3,exp:'Michels\' **iron law of oligarchy** says every organization ends up ruled by a small elite. McDonaldization describes efficiency, calculability, predictability and control spreading through society instead.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'A _____ is a religious group that arose from a split from a larger religion',choices:['denomination','ecclesia','cult','sect'],ans:3,exp:'A **sect** breaks away from a parent religion, often in protest, and can grow into a denomination over time. A cult (new religious movement) is new and not derived from an existing religion.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'The basic model of emotional expression states that emotions and their facial expressions are _____',choices:['learned entirely from culture','unique to each individual','determined by language','universal'],ans:3,exp:'The **basic model** (Darwin, Ekman) holds that emotions and their facial expressions are biologically based and **universal** across cultures. The social construction model argues the opposite: emotions are learned from culture.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'The social construction model of emotional expression states that there is no biological circuitry for emotions, since emotions are based solely on _____',choices:['genetic programming','universal facial expressions','hormonal states','experience and context'],ans:3,exp:'The **social construction model** says emotions come from social **experience and context**, so feelings like shame differ between cultures. The basic model counters that some emotions are universal.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ are unspoken cultural rules that govern how and when emotions may be expressed',choices:['Basic emotions','Impression management','Facial feedback','Display rules'],ans:3,exp:'**Display rules** vary by culture and gender (e.g., masking disappointment over a gift). They explain why universal emotions are expressed differently across cultures.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ refers to the maintenance of a public image, which is accomplished through various strategies',choices:['Display rules','Role performance','Self-verification','Impression management'],ans:3,exp:'**Impression management** strategies include self-disclosure, managing appearances, ingratiation, aligning actions and alter-casting. Self-verification is the opposite motive: seeking feedback that confirms one\'s existing self-view.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'The _____ to social interaction says people create images of themselves in the same way that actors perform a role in front of an audience',choices:['functionalist approach','conflict approach','exchange-rational choice approach','dramaturgical approach'],ans:3,exp:'Goffman\'s **dramaturgical approach** separates the **front stage** (the public performance) from the **back stage** (where people relax out of role). Functionalist and conflict approaches look at whole societies, not individual self-presentation.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is communication through spoken, written, or signed words',choices:['Nonverbal communication','Paralanguage','Body language','Verbal communication'],ans:3,exp:'**Verbal communication** uses words, including sign language. Nonverbal communication (gestures, facial expressions) and paralanguage (tone, pitch, pace) carry meaning without words.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is communication through means other than words',choices:['Verbal communication','Sign language','Written communication','Nonverbal communication'],ans:3,exp:'**Nonverbal communication** includes facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact and paralanguage (tone, pitch). Sign language uses words, so it counts as verbal communication.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'Animals use body language, facial expressions, visual displays, scents, and vocalizations to _____',choices:['thermoregulate','hibernate','migrate','communicate'],ans:3,exp:'**Animal communication** passes information about threats, mating readiness, food and territory; examples are the honeybee waggle dance and pheromone trails. These signals are mostly innate but can be shaped by learning.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the attraction between people that leads to the development of platonic or romantic relationships',choices:['Social attachment','Social support','Altruism','Interpersonal attraction'],ans:3,exp:'**Interpersonal attraction** increases with proximity (the mere-exposure effect), similarity, reciprocal liking and physical attractiveness. Social attachment is the lasting emotional bond that can develop afterward.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is physical, verbal, or nonverbal behavior intended to cause harm or increase social dominance',choices:['Assertiveness','Competition','Deindividuation','Aggression'],ans:3,exp:'**Aggression** is linked to amygdala activity, low prefrontal control, testosterone and frustration (the frustration-aggression hypothesis). What separates it from assertiveness is the **intent to harm**.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is an emotional bond to another person',choices:['Interpersonal attraction','Social support','Social capital','Social attachment'],ans:3,exp:'**Social attachment** begins with the infant-caregiver bond, studied by Bowlby, Harlow (the monkeys preferred a soft cloth mother) and Ainsworth (the Strange Situation). The attachment style formed in infancy influences later relationships.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is when a child clearly prefers the caregiver to strangers, is upset when the caregiver leaves, and is comforted when the caregiver returns',choices:['Avoidant attachment','Ambivalent attachment','Disorganized attachment','Secure attachment'],ans:3,exp:'**Secure attachment** comes from consistent, responsive caregiving; the child uses the caregiver as a secure base to explore from. It is the most common pattern in Ainsworth\'s Strange Situation.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is when a child shows no preference for the caregiver compared to strangers',choices:['Secure attachment','Ambivalent attachment','Disorganized attachment','Avoidant attachment'],ans:3,exp:'**Avoidant attachment** develops when caregivers are unresponsive or rejecting; the child shows little distress at separation and ignores the caregiver on return. In ambivalent attachment, by contrast, the child is very distressed.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ leads a child to become distressed when a caregiver leaves and to be ambivalent when the caregiver returns',choices:['Secure attachment','Avoidant attachment','Disorganized attachment','Ambivalent attachment'],ans:3,exp:'**Ambivalent (anxious-resistant) attachment** comes from inconsistent caregiving; the child seeks comfort but resists it at reunion. A secure child is easily comforted on return.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is when a child shows no clear pattern of behavior in response to the caregiver\'s absence or presence',choices:['Secure attachment','Avoidant attachment','Ambivalent attachment','Disorganized attachment'],ans:3,exp:'**Disorganized attachment** involves erratic behavior such as freezing or approaching and then avoiding the caregiver. It is strongly linked to abuse or frightening caregiving.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the perception or reality that one is cared for by a social network',choices:['Social capital','Social perception','Social facilitation','Social support'],ans:3,exp:'**Social support** (emotional, esteem, material, informational or network) buffers stress and is linked to better health outcomes. Social capital refers to the resources that relationships give access to, not the feeling of being cared for.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is listening to, affirming, and empathizing with someone\'s feelings',choices:['Esteem support','Informational support','Material support','Emotional support'],ans:3,exp:'**Emotional support** focuses on feelings (e.g., "That sounds really hard"). Esteem support targets a person\'s abilities and qualities instead.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ affirms the qualities and skills of the person',choices:['Emotional support','Informational support','Network support','Esteem support'],ans:3,exp:'**Esteem support** boosts confidence (e.g., "You\'re a great problem-solver; you can handle this"). Emotional support validates feelings rather than abilities.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is providing physical or monetary help',choices:['Emotional support','Esteem support','Informational support','Material support'],ans:3,exp:'**Material (tangible) support** includes money, meals, rides or other practical help. Informational support gives advice or knowledge instead.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is providing useful information to a person',choices:['Emotional support','Material support','Network support','Informational support'],ans:3,exp:'**Informational support** includes advice, suggestions and facts that help someone deal with a problem. Material support is practical help such as money or rides.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is providing a person with a sense of belonging',choices:['Emotional support','Esteem support','Informational support','Network support'],ans:3,exp:'**Network support** gives a sense of group membership, such as a support group of people with the same condition. Emotional support validates feelings rather than giving a sense of belonging.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is searching for and exploiting food resources',choices:['Mate choice','Altruism','Territoriality','Foraging'],ans:3,exp:'**Foraging** behavior is shaped by both genes and learning. Optimal foraging theory predicts that animals maximize energy gained relative to the cost and risk of getting it.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ describe the way a group is organized in terms of sexual behavior',choices:['Kinship systems','Dominance hierarchies','Foraging strategies','Mating systems'],ans:3,exp:'**Mating systems** include monogamy, polygamy (polygyny or polyandry) and promiscuity. They are shaped by how much each parent invests in offspring and by how resources are distributed.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ describes exclusive mating relationships between one male and one female',choices:['Polygyny','Polyandry','Promiscuity','Monogamy'],ans:3,exp:'**Monogamy** is common in species where both parents must care for the young, such as many birds. It is rare among mammals.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the general term for one member of a sex having multiple exclusive relationships with members of the opposite sex',choices:['Monogamy','Promiscuity','Mate choice','Polygamy'],ans:3,exp:'**Polygamy** has two forms: **polygyny** (one male, multiple females) and **polyandry** (one female, multiple males). Promiscuity involves no exclusive relationships at all.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the mating system that describes one male with multiple females',choices:['Polyandry','Monogamy','Promiscuity','Polygyny'],ans:3,exp:'**Polygyny** ("many women") is the most common form of polygamy, as in a gorilla harem. Mnemonic: gyn = female (as in gynecology), andr = male (as in androgen).'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the mating system that describes one female with multiple males',choices:['Polygyny','Monogamy','Promiscuity','Polyandry'],ans:3,exp:'**Polyandry** ("many men") is rare; examples include some shorebirds and certain Himalayan human societies. Polygyny is the reverse: one male with multiple females.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the mating system characterized by no exclusivity',choices:['Monogamy','Polygyny','Polyandry','Promiscuity'],ans:3,exp:'In **promiscuity**, either sex mates with multiple partners without forming pair bonds (e.g., chimpanzees and bonobos). Polygyny and polyandry still involve exclusive relationships.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the selection of a mate based on attraction and traits',choices:['Inclusive fitness','Promiscuity','Altruism','Mate choice'],ans:3,exp:'**Mate choice** (intersexual selection) means one sex, often females, chooses partners based on traits that signal good genes or resources, such as the peacock\'s tail. Inclusive fitness measures genetic success through oneself and relatives, not partner selection.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is the belief that the well-being of others is equally, if not more, important than one\'s own well-being or survival',choices:['Egoism','Social loafing','Game theory','Altruism'],ans:3,exp:'**Altruism** means helping others at a cost to oneself. Evolution explains it through **inclusive fitness** (kin selection) and reciprocal altruism.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ attempts to explain decision-making between individuals as if they are participating in a game',choices:['Inclusive fitness','Attribution theory','Signal detection theory','Game theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Game theory** models strategic interaction; the prisoner\'s dilemma and the hawk-dove game show when cooperation or altruism can be advantageous.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a measure of an organism\'s success based on how well its genes are propagated, both through its own offspring and through helping relatives reproduce',choices:['Natural selection','Altruism','Mate choice','Inclusive fitness'],ans:3,exp:'**Inclusive fitness** explains why animals help relatives who share their genes (Hamilton\'s rule: rB > C). Altruism is the helping behavior itself, while inclusive fitness is how its evolutionary benefit is measured.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the way we form impressions about people in our social environment',choices:['Social facilitation','Social support','Social capital','Social perception'],ans:3,exp:'**Social perception** (social cognition) involves a perceiver, a target and a situation, and it is shaped by primacy, recency and the halo effect. Social facilitation is about how others\' presence changes our performance, not our impressions of them.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'10A',q:'_____ refers to the resources gained by developing and maintaining relationships that form social networks willing to help each other',choices:['Social support','Social perception','Social facilitation','Social capital'],ans:3,exp:'**Social capital** is the benefit people get from their networks, such as job leads through "weak ties". Social support is the feeling or reality of being cared for, not access to resources.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ states that when we first meet someone, we notice one of their characteristics and then assume other traits about the person based on it',choices:['Attribution theory','Correspondent inference theory','Just-world hypothesis','Implicit personality theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Implicit personality theory** says we hold built-in assumptions about which traits go together (e.g., "warm people are generous"). It underlies stereotyping and the halo effect.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ are systematic errors in reasoning, evaluating, remembering, or other cognitive processes, often resulting from holding onto one\'s preferences and beliefs despite contrary information',choices:['Heuristics','Attributions','Schemas','Cognitive biases'],ans:3,exp:'**Cognitive biases** include confirmation bias, hindsight bias and belief perseverance. Heuristics are the shortcuts themselves, which can produce biases but are not errors by definition.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ focuses on how individuals infer the causes of other people\'s behavior',choices:['Implicit personality theory','Social exchange theory','Elaboration likelihood model','Attribution theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Attribution theory** (Heider) separates dispositional (internal) causes from situational (external) ones, and Kelley added consistency, distinctiveness and consensus. Implicit personality theory is about inferring traits, not causes.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the assumption that a person\'s behavior reflects their internal dispositions, such as personality, beliefs, or attitudes',choices:['Situational attribution','Fundamental attribution error','Actor-observer bias','Dispositional attribution'],ans:3,exp:'A **dispositional (internal) attribution** explains behavior by the person\'s traits (e.g., "she\'s late because she\'s lazy"). The fundamental attribution error is the bias of overusing it for other people.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the assumption that a person\'s behavior is due to situational factors',choices:['Dispositional attribution','Fundamental attribution error','Actor-observer bias','Situational attribution'],ans:3,exp:'A **situational (external) attribution** explains behavior by circumstances (e.g., "she\'s late because of traffic"). People tend to use it for their own behavior more than for others\'.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the bias toward making dispositional rather than situational attributions about the actions of others',choices:['Actor-observer bias','Self-serving bias','Halo effect','Fundamental attribution error'],ans:3,exp:'The **fundamental attribution error** is stronger in individualist cultures, while collectivist cultures weigh situations more. The actor-observer bias adds the contrast with oneself, explaining one\'s own behavior situationally.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ states that observers assume a person\'s behavior matches, or corresponds with, that person\'s personality traits',choices:['Implicit personality theory','Attribute substitution','Self-fulfilling prophecy','Correspondent inference theory'],ans:3,exp:'**Correspondent inference theory** (Jones and Davis) says we infer a matching trait from behavior, especially when the behavior is freely chosen and unexpected. Overusing such inferences produces the fundamental attribution error (correspondence bias).'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is when individuals must make a complex judgment but instead substitute a simpler judgment or heuristic',choices:['Belief perseverance','Fundamental attribution error','Anchoring bias','Attribute substitution'],ans:3,exp:'In **attribute substitution** (Kahneman and Frederick), a hard question (e.g., "How likely is this?") is unconsciously replaced with an easier one (e.g., "How easily do examples come to mind?"). It underlies many heuristics and is unrelated to attribution, the explaining of the causes of behavior.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the tendency to attribute your own actions to external causes and other people\'s actions to dispositional causes',choices:['Self-serving bias','Fundamental attribution error','Optimism bias','Actor-observer bias'],ans:3,exp:'The **actor-observer bias** happens because, as actors, we see the situation, while as observers we see the person. Self-serving bias differs: credit for success is internal and blame for failure is external.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ are attitudes and impressions formed from limited and superficial information about a group',choices:['Discrimination','Ethnocentrism','Stigma','Stereotypes'],ans:3,exp:'**Stereotypes** are the cognitive part of bias (beliefs about a group). Prejudice is the affective part (feelings), and discrimination is the behavioral part (actions).'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'A _____ is when stereotypes lead to expectations, and those expectations create conditions that confirm the stereotype',choices:['stereotype threat','just-world hypothesis','confirmation bias','self-fulfilling prophecy'],ans:3,exp:'In a **self-fulfilling prophecy**, others\' expectations change behavior until the expectation comes true (e.g., the Pygmalion effect with teachers). Stereotype threat is a person\'s own anxiety about confirming a stereotype, not other people\'s expectations shaping events.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is when people feel at risk of confirming negative stereotypes about their social group, which can impair their performance',choices:['Self-fulfilling prophecy','Prejudice','Social loafing','Stereotype threat'],ans:3,exp:'**Stereotype threat** (Steele and Aronson) causes anxiety that lowers performance, for example on tests where a group stereotype is made salient. A self-fulfilling prophecy works through other people\'s expectations instead.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is an irrational positive or negative attitude toward a person, group, or thing formed before actual experience with it',choices:['Stereotype','Discrimination','Ethnocentrism','Prejudice'],ans:3,exp:'**Prejudice** is the affective (attitude) part of bias. Stereotypes are the beliefs behind it, and discrimination is the behavior that may follow.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is when prejudicial attitudes cause individuals of a particular group to be treated differently from others',choices:['Prejudice','Stereotyping','Ethnocentrism','Discrimination'],ans:3,exp:'**Discrimination** is the behavioral part of bias and can be individual or institutional. A person can be prejudiced without discriminating, and vice versa.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the practice of judging other cultures based on the values and beliefs of one\'s own culture',choices:['Cultural relativism','Xenocentrism','Assimilation','Ethnocentrism'],ans:3,exp:'**Ethnocentrism** treats one\'s own culture as the standard and can foster in-group bias. Xenocentrism is the opposite: the belief that another culture is superior to one\'s own.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8B',q:'_____ is the idea that a person\'s beliefs, values, and practices should be understood based on that person\'s own culture, rather than judged against the criteria of a different culture',choices:['Ethnocentrism','Xenocentrism','Assimilation','Cultural relativism'],ans:3,exp:'**Cultural relativism** is the standard approach in sociology and anthropology for studying other cultures. It does not require approving of every practice, only understanding it in its context.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'GABA inhibits a neuron by allowing _____ to enter the neuron, which _____ the cell',choices:['Na⁺ / depolarizes','K⁺ / depolarizes','Cl⁻ / hyperpolarizes','Ca²⁺ / hyperpolarizes'],ans:2,exp:'**GABA-A** receptors are ligand-gated **Cl⁻** channels; Cl⁻ influx makes the membrane potential more negative (**hyperpolarization**), moving the neuron away from threshold. Na⁺ influx would depolarize the cell, which is how excitatory transmitters work.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Glutamate allows _____ to enter the neuron, which _____ the cell and can trigger an action potential',choices:['Cl⁻ / hyperpolarizes','K⁺ / hyperpolarizes','Cl⁻ / depolarizes','Na⁺ / depolarizes'],ans:3,exp:'Glutamate opens ionotropic **AMPA and NMDA** receptors, letting **Na⁺** (and Ca²⁺) in; this cation influx **depolarizes** the cell toward threshold. Cl⁻ influx is the inhibitory GABA mechanism.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'7A',q:'Cortisol is released in response to stress and low blood glucose. It increases blood sugar by _____',choices:['stimulating insulin release','promoting glycogen synthesis','increasing glucose uptake by muscle','activating gluconeogenesis'],ans:3,exp:'**Cortisol** promotes **gluconeogenesis** in the liver (making glucose from amino acids and glycerol) and breaks down protein and fat to supply it. Insulin release and glycogen synthesis both lower blood sugar, so they work against cortisol.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'Recognizing information is _____ than recalling it',choices:['harder','slower','less accurate','easier'],ans:3,exp:'**Recognition** (picking the right answer from options) is easier than free **recall** because the cue is supplied, so less retrieval effort is needed. This is why multiple-choice items feel easier than fill-in-the-blank ones.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'In Piaget\'s _____, the child manipulates the environment to meet physical needs through circular reactions',choices:['preoperational stage (2–7)','concrete operational stage (7–11)','formal operational stage (12+)','sensorimotor stage (0–2)'],ans:3,exp:'In the **sensorimotor stage**, primary circular reactions (repeating body actions such as thumb-sucking) give way to secondary ones (repeating actions on objects), and object permanence emerges. Symbolic thought begins in the preoperational stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s _____ is characterized by pretend play, symbolic thinking, egocentrism, and centration',choices:['sensorimotor stage (0–2)','concrete operational stage (7–11)','formal operational stage (12+)','preoperational stage (2–7)'],ans:3,exp:'In the **preoperational stage**, **centration** (focusing on one feature, such as the height of a glass) prevents conservation, and **egocentrism** prevents taking others\' perspectives. Both are overcome in the concrete operational stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s _____ is characterized by understanding others\' feelings, conservation, and mathematical reasoning about concrete objects',choices:['sensorimotor stage (0–2)','preoperational stage (2–7)','formal operational stage (12+)','concrete operational stage (7–11)'],ans:3,exp:'In the **concrete operational stage**, children grasp **conservation** and reversibility and can take others\' perspectives, but their logic is still tied to concrete things. Abstract reasoning comes in the formal operational stage.'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'6B',q:'Piaget\'s _____ is characterized by abstract thought, systematic problem solving, and moral reasoning',choices:['sensorimotor stage (0–2)','preoperational stage (2–7)','concrete operational stage (7–11)','formal operational stage (12+)'],ans:3,exp:'In the **formal operational stage**, adolescents reason about hypotheticals and abstract ideas, as shown by Piaget\'s pendulum task. Concrete operational thinkers can reason only about tangible objects.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'During N2 sleep, the background EEG consists mainly of _____ waves, interrupted by sleep spindles and K complexes',choices:['alpha','beta','delta','theta'],ans:3,exp:'**N2** keeps the **theta** background of N1 and adds **sleep spindles** and **K complexes**. Delta waves do not dominate until N3.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'During N3 (slow-wave) sleep, _____ waves appear on EEG',choices:['theta','alpha','beta','delta'],ans:3,exp:'**N3**, which merged the older stages 3 and 4, is dominated by high-amplitude, low-frequency **delta** waves. It is the deepest sleep stage and is linked to growth hormone release, sleepwalking and night terrors.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'During REM sleep, the EEG mainly shows _____, resembling an awake brain',choices:['delta waves','sleep spindles','K complexes','beta waves'],ans:3,exp:'**REM** sleep shows fast, low-voltage, **beta-like** activity much like wakefulness, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep. (Strictly, REM EEG is a mixed-frequency pattern with theta and sawtooth waves; "beta" is the standard MCAT shorthand.) Sleep spindles and K complexes mark N2, and delta waves mark N3.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ triggers sleepiness',choices:['Cortisol','Orexin (hypocretin)','Norepinephrine','Melatonin'],ans:3,exp:'**Melatonin** is secreted by the pineal gland in darkness under control of the suprachiasmatic nucleus, signaling that it is time to sleep. Orexin promotes wakefulness, and its loss causes narcolepsy.'},
  {s:'Biological Bases of Behavior',aamc:'6B',q:'_____ rises in the early morning and promotes wakefulness',choices:['Melatonin','Adenosine','GABA','Cortisol'],ans:3,exp:'**Cortisol** peaks shortly after waking (the **cortisol awakening response**) and promotes alertness. Melatonin, adenosine (which builds up the longer one is awake; caffeine blocks it) and GABA all promote sleep.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'Compared to their performance alone, in the presence of others people tend to perform _____ on simple or well-rehearsed tasks and _____ on complex or new tasks',choices:['worse / better','better / better','worse / worse','better / worse'],ans:3,exp:'**Social facilitation**: the presence of others raises arousal, which helps dominant, well-learned responses but hurts performance on difficult or new tasks, consistent with the Yerkes-Dodson law.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'7B',q:'A(n) _____ is an official reward or punishment',choices:['informal sanction','folkway','taboo','formal sanction'],ans:3,exp:'**Formal sanctions** are imposed by institutions, such as a court fine, a diploma or a promotion. Informal sanctions come from everyday social interaction (e.g., a frown or praise).'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'8C',q:'_____ is a type of society that emphasizes secondary relationships based on impersonal ties',choices:['Gemeinschaft','Bureaucracy','Primary group','Gesellschaft'],ans:3,exp:'Tönnies\' **Gesellschaft** ("society") is modern, urban and based on self-interest and formal roles. Gemeinschaft ("community") is based on close personal and family ties instead.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'Primary drives are related to _____',choices:['learned associations','social approval','cultural values','biological needs'],ans:3,exp:'**Primary drives** (hunger, thirst, temperature regulation, sex) arise from **biological needs** and maintain homeostasis, largely through the hypothalamus. Secondary drives are learned.'},
  {s:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',aamc:'7A',q:'Secondary drives stem from _____',choices:['biological needs','genetic programming','homeostasis','learning'],ans:3,exp:'**Secondary drives** (e.g., for money, achievement or approval) are acquired through **learning**, often by association with primary drives. Biological needs give rise to primary drives instead.'},
  {s:'Sensation & Perception',aamc:'6A',q:'Rhodopsin is a light-sensitive pigment found in the _____ of the retina',choices:['ganglion cells','bipolar cells','cones','rods'],ans:3,exp:'**Rhodopsin** in **rods** is very sensitive to light, which is why rods handle night vision; light converts 11-cis-retinal to all-trans-retinal and starts the phototransduction cascade. Cones use different pigments (photopsins).'},
  {s:'Personality, Development & Disorders',aamc:'7A',q:'_____ symptoms of schizophrenia are the absence of normal, healthy behaviors, such as disturbances of affect and avolition',choices:['Positive','Prodromal','Catatonic','Negative'],ans:3,exp:'**Negative** symptoms include flat affect, avolition, alogia (poverty of speech), anhedonia and asociality. They respond poorly to typical antipsychotics, while positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) respond better.'},
  {s:'Social Psychology & Society',aamc:'9A',q:'According to _____, society is a system of interconnected parts that work together in harmony to maintain a state of balance and social equilibrium for the whole',choices:['conflict theory','symbolic interactionism','social constructionism','functionalism'],ans:3,exp:'**Functionalism** (Durkheim, Parsons) examines the manifest (intended) and latent (unintended) functions of institutions. Conflict theory sees society as a competition for limited resources rather than a balanced system.'},
];
